window.COURSE_CONTENT = {"classes": [{"slug": "class-1-its-just-numbers", "sourceFile": "01-class-1-its-just-numbers.md", "title": "Class 1: It's Just Numbers, Honest", "subtitle": "What AI actually is, what it isn't, and why the difference matters to you", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 15-minute exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** explain in two plain sentences what an AI model is, tell a model apart from a product like ChatGPT, list three tasks these tools are reliably good at today and three they are not, and name the economic change that matters most to a small business.\n\n## Start with something you have already used\n\nYour phone keyboard guesses your next word as you type. It is rarely magical and often right, because it has seen enough of your sentences to know their shape. A large language model (the \"LL\" in LLM, a model trained on vast amounts of text) is that same idea at enormous scale. Instead of learning from your last three words, it learns patterns from more written material than any human could read in a lifetime. Hold that picture. Nearly everything in this course follows from it.\n\n## What a model actually is\n\nA model is a stack of numbers: billions of them, arranged in a specific structure, and that is it. A useful way to picture that stack is a mixing desk with billions of faders. During training, a computer programme nudges those faders a hair at a time, checks how good the guess was, and nudges again, millions of times, until the guesses line up with the examples it was shown. Then the faders are locked. The finished model is simply that desk: a file of billions of numbers in a fixed arrangement.\n\nWhen you use the model, your text runs across those numbers at speed and out comes the most plausible continuation. There is no mind in the file and no ghost in the machine. When someone says the model \"thinks\" or \"understands\", they are reaching for a metaphor, and metaphors are fine in conversation as long as you know that is all they are.\n\n## Why confident nonsense happens\n\nThe model is tuned to produce plausible text, and plausible is not the same as true. Where its reading contains plenty of examples of what an answer looks like but nothing reliable about your exact question, it produces the shape of an answer: a confident sentence, a realistic-looking reference, a number in the right range. The industry calls this a hallucination (output that is confident, plausible and wrong). Plain English is better: the model made it up. This one fact explains most AI horror stories, and it is why every later class in this course ends with a human checking names, numbers, dates and quotes.\n\n## Tokens, in one paragraph\n\nModels read in mouthfuls of text called tokens (chunks of roughly three-quarters of an English word). You will never need to count them. You need the concept because prices, speed and memory limits are all measured in tokens, and because Class 4 uses it to explain why a long conversation gets worse near the end.\n\n## The engine and the car\n\nThe model is the engine. ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and Copilot are cars: software products built around an engine, complete with a user interface, a billing system, a terms-of-service agreement and a company trying to make money. Two cars can share an engine, and one company can swap the engine under its car without much ceremony. The practical payoff for you is consumer power. Cars can be test-driven, compared, cancelled and negotiated. Keep the engine in mind as an implementation detail and judge the car on what it does with your work.\n\n## What these tools are good at today\n\n- Drafting and rewriting text of almost any kind, from emails to policy documents.\n- Summarising long material and pulling structure out of messy notes.\n- Translating jargon into plain language, in both directions.\n- First drafts of spreadsheets formulas, checklists and code.\n- Brainstorming options when you need fifteen ideas and have three.\n\nNotice the pattern: every one of these is a task where a rough first draft saves time and a human checks the result. That is the sweet spot, and it is a large one.\n\n## What they are bad at today\n\n- Reliable facts, unless you supply them or the product fetches them with a live search you can inspect.\n- Arithmetic across many steps. Ask for a formula or a small program instead, then check it.\n- Knowing anything about your business that you have not told them in this conversation or stored setting.\n- Admitting uncertainty. These tools do not say \"I don't know\" unless they were tuned to.\n\n## The quiet story that matters most\n\nIn 1995, custom software meant developers, servers, licences and physical media, so it was built once and sold to thousands, and a ten-person business bent its processes around whatever was shrink-wrapped. Copying and running software is now effectively free at the margin (the cost of one extra copy or one extra user is close to zero), and AI tools now write much of the routine code. Software tailored to your one specific business, which used to be a six-figure conversation, can now be an afternoon. Class 5 is entirely about this, because it is the change that will actually show up in your budget.\n\n## Cloud or local, the thirty-second version\n\nMost products run in the cloud (on the vendor's servers, with your text travelling to them over the internet). Some models publish their numbers so you can run them on your own machine, which is called running locally. Cloud buys convenience and scale. Local buys control and privacy, plus a maintenance job. Class 3 turns this into a decision you can make about your own data.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"AI understands language.\"** It produces language that reads as if understanding sits behind it, because the patterns it absorbed were written by people who did understand. When it explains a tax rule it is not consulting a concept of tax. It is reproducing the shape of text that tax explanations take, which is why it is brilliant on the common case and badly wrong on the unusual one. Treat every output as fluent pattern-matching, not as understanding, and you have the single habit that prevents most AI-related business disasters.\n\n## Your exercise: two products, one task\n\n1. Write one real work task as a request. For example: draft a polite reminder email to a customer who is 30 days overdue on an invoice of $2,300.\n2. Paste the identical request into two different products, using free accounts if that is all you have. Nothing sensitive goes in.\n3. Compare the two answers for length, tone, confidence, and any invented detail such as a fake interest rate or deadline. Note which one you would send with less editing.\n4. Add one hard constraint, such as \"maximum 80 words, no apologies, mention that work continues on schedule\", and run both again. Note how much the answers improve.\n5. Write one sentence answering this: was the difference between the products bigger, or the difference made by your constraint? Most people find the constraint wins, and that finding is Class 4 in miniature.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can explain what a model is without saying \"it thinks\".\n- I can name the product I use most and say which company's engine likely sits under it.\n- I can list one task I will try this week and one task I will keep fully human for now.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Model:** the trained file of numbers that does the pattern-matching. The engine.\n- **Product or app:** the software around a model that you actually use, such as ChatGPT or Copilot. The car.\n- **Token:** a mouthful of text, roughly three-quarters of an English word. The unit prices and limits are measured in.\n- **Prompt:** the text you type to the model.\n- **System prompt:** standing instructions placed before your prompt that shape tone, format and rules. Class 4 and Document 1 cover writing your own.\n- **Context window:** the maximum text a model can consider at once, like the size of its desk.\n- **Hallucination:** confident, plausible, wrong output. The model made it up.\n- **Training:** the one-off, expensive process of setting the model's numbers using large datasets. It happens at the vendor, long before you type anything.\n- **Weights:** another word for the model's numbers. \"Open weights\" means published, so anyone can run the model on their own hardware.\n- **Local model:** a model running on your computer or office server instead of a vendor's cloud."}, {"slug": "class-2-selling-the-sizzle", "sourceFile": "02-class-2-selling-the-sizzle.md", "title": "Class 2: Selling the Sizzle", "subtitle": "How to read AI marketing without losing your lunch or your budget", "md": "\n**Time:** about 25 minutes of reading, plus a 15-minute exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** apply a two-line field test to any AI claim, recognise five marketing tells by name, run a vendor demo like a buyer, and use ten questions that separate capability from brochure.\n\n## The field test, in two lines\n\nIf a claim requires you to imagine a future that has not arrived yet, it is marketing. If a claim describes what the software does today, on your screen, with your data, right now, it is probably real.\n\nThat is the whole tool. \"Our agents will handle your entire customer service operation\" is the first kind. \"Paste in this 40-page contract and it produces a clause-by-clause summary in about a minute\" is the second kind, and you can verify it yourself before lunch. Everything else in this class is practice at applying those two lines under pressure, in a meeting, with a confident person on the other side of the table.\n\n## Why AI marketing is unusually thick\n\nThree reasons, all commercial, none mysterious. The vendors are in a land grab and have spent billions on datacentres, so the pitch must justify the spend. The technology genuinely improved fast between 2022 and 2024, which handed marketers a real story to inflate. And output quality varies from task to task, so a choreographed demo of the easy 20 percent can carry a purchase decision for the hard 80 percent. Knowing why the sizzle exists makes it easier to hear past it without becoming cynical about the genuinely useful product underneath.\n\n## Demos are theatre. Pilots are evidence.\n\nVendor demos are choreographed: pre-selected prompts, forgiving tasks, retries hidden. The counter-move is a pilot test. You supply the data and the task, on your material, in a session you control, with a success measure agreed in writing before anyone opens a laptop. If a vendor refuses a pilot on your data, treat the refusal as the answer. It is the single most informative sentence a salesperson can say.\n\n## Five case studies, and what each one teaches\n\n**Deloitte and the refund (October 2025).** Deloitte Australia delivered a report to the federal Department of Home Affairs, reportedly worth about $440,000, which reviewers found contained AI-generated errors including invented references and fabricated quotations attributed to real academics. Deloitte admitted AI tools were used contrary to its own contractual terms and refunded part of the payment. The lesson is not \"consultants are careless\". It is that a global firm with every resource available still shipped confident nonsense, and the error was caught by a human reader who checked the citations. Checking is the product you are actually paying for.\n\n**Klarna's 700 agents (claimed 2024, walked back 2025).** The Swedish fintech announced its AI assistant was doing the work of 700 full-time customer-service agents. By May 2025 its chief executive was publicly admitting the quality push had gone too far and that human staff were coming back. The lesson: \"handles the volume of 700 agents\" is a marketing figure with no published method, and cost cutting advertised as AI transformation often turns out to be plain cost cutting. Analysts now have a term for dressing ordinary cuts in AI language: AI washing.\n\n**Air Canada's chatbot (tribunal decision, February 2024).** A customer asked the airline's website chatbot about bereavement fares, received wrong information, and the airline then argued the chatbot was a separate legal entity responsible for its own actions. The tribunal rejected that argument and ordered the airline to pay. The lesson belongs on your office wall: whatever your chatbot tells a customer, your business owns it.\n\n**The regulators have a word for this (March 2024, United States).** The Securities and Exchange Commission fined two investment advisers, Delphia and Global Predictions, US$225,000 and US$175,000 for claiming AI capabilities they did not have. Australian regulators have signalled the same scrutiny under existing consumer law. The lesson: exaggerated AI claims are becoming an enforcement category, which means you can ask a vendor what happens to their claims if a regulator tests them, and watch the face.\n\n**McDonald's drive-through (trial ended June 2024).** The chain ended a trial of automated order-taking after viral videos of the system adding bacon to ice cream and hundreds of dollars of nuggets to ordinary orders. The lesson: public-facing AI failures are filmed, and the reputational cost lands on your brand, not the vendor's.\n\n## The sizzle vocabulary, translated on the spot\n\n- **Agentic:** the software attempts multi-step tasks with some autonomy. Reliability varies wildly by task, so ask to see your task.\n- **PhD-level or expert-level:** a result on one narrow benchmark (a standard test used to compare models), not a job qualification.\n- **10x productivity:** a multiple with no stated baseline. Ask \"ten times what, measured how, by whom?\"\n- **Enterprise-grade:** a pricing tier, not a quality guarantee.\n- **Powered by AI:** sometimes means one summarisation feature bolted onto an ordinary product. Ask which feature, exactly.\n- **Seamlessly integrates:** ask which system, which version, and for a reference customer you may call.\n\n## Future-tense tells\n\nWatch the grammar. Heavy use of \"will\", \"soon\", \"on our roadmap\" (roadmap meaning features that do not exist yet) and \"imagine a world\" is the sound of a brochure. Ask the vendor to repeat every \"will\" sentence as a present-tense sentence about today's product. Whatever survives that exercise is the product you can buy. The rest is a hope with a price tag.\n\n## Take these ten questions to your next demo\n\n1. Show me my data in your tool, not your sample data.\n2. What exactly happens to data I upload, and where is it stored?\n3. Do you train on my data, and which pricing tier changes that answer?\n4. What is the failure mode (the characteristic way this tool goes wrong) and who catches it?\n5. Which integration is real today and which is roadmap?\n6. Name two customers my size in my industry I can call.\n7. What does your contract say about liability for wrong output?\n8. What is the total first-year cost, including seats, minimums and integration work?\n9. What happens to my data and my workflows if I cancel?\n10. What did you claim last year that turned out to be wrong?\n\nQuestion ten is the character test. The answers to it are reliably revealing, and a vendor who answers it well has earned the rest of the meeting.\n\n## Point the test inward\n\nThe same discipline applies to your own building. When a staff member says AI will save ten hours a week, ask which task, measured how, verified when. When your board paper says \"we are adopting AI\", replace the sentence with one claim about today's capability and see if it still stands. Class 6 turns this habit into paperwork your team can actually use.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"If a big, reputable company says it, the capability must be real.\"** Reputation and accuracy are different products. Deloitte is one of the most established professional-services brands on earth and still refunded part of a government report over invented citations. Klarna's chief executive personally announced the 700-agent figure and personally walked it back. Air Canada's lawyers argued a chatbot was a separate legal entity, on the record, and lost. None of these organisations is stupid. All of them repeated confident AI output or an inflated AI story because it suited a narrative, a share price or a sales cycle. A claim's credibility comes from the evidence behind it, never from the size of the logo beside it.\n\n## Your exercise: roast one brochure\n\n1. Find one piece of real AI marketing: a vendor email, a product page, a flyer, a sponsored post.\n2. Underline every sentence that describes verifiable behaviour today. Circle every sentence that requires imagining the future.\n3. Rewrite your three most circled sentences as honest present-tense claims.\n4. Write the three hardest questions from the ten above that this material conspicuously avoids answering.\n5. Decide, in one sentence: would this vendor survive a pilot test on your data? Notice that your honest rewrites are usually still sellable. Real capability does not need the sizzle, which is exactly why the sizzle is a signal.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can state the field test from memory in two lines.\n- I can name two marketing tells I have personally heard in the last month.\n- I have ten questions saved somewhere I will actually find them before the next demo.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **AI washing:** describing a product, process or layoff as AI-driven when the AI element is minimal, unproven or absent. Now also a regulatory enforcement category.\n- **Benchmark:** a standard test used to compare models. Useful for vendors, narrow for buyers.\n- **Pilot test:** a short, agreed, measurable trial on your own data and tasks before purchase.\n- **Agentic AI:** software that attempts multi-step tasks with some autonomy instead of answering one prompt. Treat every agentic claim as unproven until demonstrated on your workflow.\n- **Roadmap:** vendor language for features that do not exist yet. Never pay for a roadmap.\n- **Failure mode:** the characteristic way a tool goes wrong. A mature vendor can describe theirs plainly.\n- **Total cost of ownership:** seats plus minimums plus integration plus training plus the staff time spent checking output. The number that belongs in your budget."}, {"slug": "class-3-your-data-their-servers", "sourceFile": "03-class-3-your-data-their-servers.md", "title": "Class 3: Your Data, Their Servers", "subtitle": "Cloud versus local AI, what \"training on your data\" means, and what Australian privacy law expects of you", "md": "\n**Time:** about 25 minutes of reading, plus a 10-minute settings exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** trace where your text goes when you type it, choose between cloud, local and contracted business tiers for a given task, switch off training on your data on each major platform, and run a three-question drill before pasting anything sensitive.\n\n## Follow one message on its journey\n\nYou type a customer's details into a chatbot and press enter. Your text travels over the internet to the vendor's servers, usually in the United States. A model running on their hardware processes it together with everything else in the conversation, and a reply travels back. On the way, your text is typically stored in your account history so you can see past chats, logged on the vendor's systems for operations and safety work, and in some circumstances readable by vendor staff or contractors reviewing flagged or sampled conversations. Later, when you delete the chat, you remove your view of it and start the vendor's deletion process. You do not make the text un-happen. Hold that journey in mind. Every rule in this class is a decision about one leg of it.\n\n## Cloud AI, stated honestly\n\nYou get convenience (nothing to install or maintain), scale (the biggest models live in datacentres, not laptops) and automatic updates. You give up control over exactly where your data sits, who at the vendor can technically access it, and what happens to it under legal process in the vendor's home country. For a small Australian business the practical risk of that last point is low, but it is not zero and it is not yours to waive, so it belongs in the decision rather than in a blind spot.\n\n## Local AI, stated honestly\n\nThe model runs on your own machine or office server. Your data never leaves the building, you can work offline, and nobody's terms of service stand between you and your files. In exchange you own the maintenance: someone chooses the model, installs updates, and notices when quality drops. And the models that fit on ordinary hardware are smaller than the frontier cloud models, so they trail them on hard reasoning while being perfectly adequate for drafting, summarising and searching your own material.\n\n## What local actually needs in 2026\n\nUseful rules of thumb. A small open-weights model (a model whose numbers are published so anyone can run it, such as Meta's Llama family, or Qwen, Mistral and Gemma models of similar size, around 7 to 9 billion parameters, where a parameter is one number in the model) runs acceptably on a machine with 16GB of memory, including an ordinary modern laptop and any Apple Silicon Mac with 16GB. A mid-size model of 12 to 32 billion parameters wants 24 to 32GB of memory or a graphics card with 12GB or more of video memory. The free tools that make this close to point-and-click are Ollama and LM Studio: install one, click a model name, and you have a private chatbox. That is genuinely the whole setup.\n\n## The hybrid pattern we recommend\n\nDo not treat this as an either-or decision, because in practice it is a sorting rule.\n\n- Cloud consumer tools, free or cheap tiers: low-sensitivity work. Marketing copy, brainstorming, summarising public documents, translating jargon.\n- Contracted business tiers, such as ChatGPT Business, Claude Team, Microsoft 365 Copilot or Google Workspace with Gemini: ordinary work data, because these tiers carry contractual promises not to train on your content and come with admin controls.\n- Local models, or a contracted tool with a signed agreement: the genuinely sensitive material. Anything under a non-disclosure agreement, health information, pre-decisional financials, employee disputes, raw client lists.\n\nMost ten-person businesses land comfortably on the middle row plus one local option for the sharp edges.\n\n## \"Training on your data\", precisely\n\nTraining means adjusting a model's numbers using text. If a vendor trains on your data, your prompts and documents may influence future versions of a model that everyone uses. What this does not mean: your client list does not become a database another user can query. Regurgitation of one small business's documents is very unlikely. The everyday risks are quieter and more certain: retention (your data sitting in vendor logs for months or years), human review (vendor staff reading sampled or flagged conversations), and exposure if the vendor is breached. Training sets the headline. Retention and review set the daily reality.\n\n## The opt-out map, consumer tiers\n\nSettings move, so treat these as the shape of the thing and re-check quarterly. The exact click-paths live in Document 5.\n\n- **ChatGPT:** Free and Plus conversations may be used to improve models unless you opt out in Settings, Data controls. Pro is excluded. Business and Enterprise are excluded by contract.\n- **Claude:** consumer chats are kept about 30 days by default, and since the August 2025 terms change, training on your chats is an opt-in. If you opt in, retention stretches to five years, so leave it off at work. Team and Enterprise content is not used for training.\n- **Gemini:** consumer prompts feed product improvement only if Gemini Apps Activity is switched on. Find it and switch it off. Workspace business prompts are not used to train models outside your organisation by default.\n- **Copilot:** consumer Copilot chats may be used to improve Microsoft's services unless you opt out in privacy settings. Anything used while signed in with a work or school account sits under commercial data protection and is not used to train foundation models.\n- **Local tools:** nothing is transmitted, so there is nothing to opt out of.\n\n## The cheapest security upgrade in this course\n\nMove staff off free consumer accounts and onto a business tier. The core of what you buy is contractual: no training on your business content, a data processing agreement, admin controls and defined retention. On consumer tiers those promises mostly do not exist. For roughly the price of a lunch per person per month, the scariest paragraph in this class stops applying to you.\n\n## What a data processing agreement is\n\nA DPA (data processing agreement, the contract clauses saying what a vendor may do with personal information you hand it: purpose limits, security measures, breach notification, deletion on request, subcontractor rules) comes with business and enterprise tiers automatically. Consumer tiers offer no DPA and no equivalent promise. If your business handles personal information and staff use AI tools for work, you need the tool's business tier with its DPA, or you need to keep sensitive data out of the tool. There is no third option that survives scrutiny.\n\n## The Australian Privacy Act, briefly and practically\n\nWho is covered: organisations with annual turnover above $3 million, plus many smaller ones regardless of size, including health service providers, businesses that trade in personal information and Commonwealth contractors. A small-business exemption still exists but has been flagged for removal in future reform rounds, so do not build a strategy on it. What matters for AI tools, in four points.\n\n1. APP 8 (the Australian Privacy Principle on cross-border disclosure): when staff paste personal information into a US-hosted chatbot, you generally remain responsible for how it is handled overseas. Contractual protections are your friend here.\n2. APP 11 (security): you must take reasonable steps to protect personal information. A written AI policy plus business-tier accounts are exactly such steps.\n3. From 10 December 2026, privacy policies must describe significant automated decision-making (decisions about people made or substantially shaped by software, AI included).\n4. A statutory tort for serious invasions of privacy has applied since June 2025, and if a vendor suffers a breach involving your personal information that risks serious harm, the Notifiable Data Breaches scheme may require you to tell the regulator, the OAIC (the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner), and the affected people.\n\nNone of this needs a lawyer for basic compliance. All of it needs one page of policy, which Class 6 and Documents 3 and 5 hand you.\n\n## The decision drill\n\nThree questions before anything goes into a tool. Does this text contain personal information, secrets, or anything under a non-disclosure agreement? If yes, is the tool on a business tier with a DPA, or running locally? If neither, rewrite the prompt to remove the sensitive parts (initials instead of names, \"a client\" instead of the client) or move to the local tool. Repeat until automatic. This drill is 90 percent of AI data hygiene.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"If I delete the chat, the data is gone.\"** Deletion removes your view, not necessarily their copies. Consumer-tier conversations typically persist in logs for a period after deletion, longer if they were selected for training or flagged for review, and deletion does nothing about copies in transit, caches or backups. On business tiers, retention is contractually defined and deletion requests are honoured in a stated timeframe, which is part of what you pay for. Treat anything you paste into a consumer tool as permanent, because you cannot reliably make it impermanent afterwards. The decision about what not to paste happens before the paste.\n\n## Your exercise: settings safari\n\n1. Open the AI account you use most, on the device you use most.\n2. Find three things and write down the click-path, because menus move: the data or privacy settings page, the toggle controlling whether conversations are used for training or product improvement, and the place to view or delete stored history.\n3. Turn the training or improvement toggle off. Read what the interface says will change.\n4. Find the temporary or no-save chat mode, if the tool has one, and run one harmless prompt through it.\n5. If this is a work account, write one question for your admin, for example: are we on a business tier where our content is excluded from training? Ask it next week. The finding of this exercise is usually the same one: the controls exist, and nobody advertises them.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can say where my text goes when I type it into my usual tool.\n- I know which tier my account is on and whether training is off.\n- I can run the decision drill out loud without reading it.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Cloud AI:** the model runs on the vendor's servers and your data travels to them.\n- **Local AI:** the model runs on your hardware and your data stays with you.\n- **Open weights:** a model whose numbers are published so anyone can download and run it, often free for businesses below a very large size.\n- **Parameter:** one of the numbers in a model. \"8B parameters\" means eight billion numbers, and more of them generally means more capability and more hardware.\n- **Ollama and LM Studio:** free programs that make running a local model close to point-and-click.\n- **Retention:** how long the vendor keeps your data even without training on it. The quieter, more certain risk.\n- **DPA:** data processing agreement, the contract defining what a vendor may do with your personal information.\n- **APP:** Australian Privacy Principle, one of the 13 rules in the Privacy Act. APP 8 covers cross-border disclosure, APP 11 covers security.\n- **Notifiable Data Breaches scheme:** the requirement to tell the OAIC and affected people about eligible breaches likely to cause serious harm, including breaches at your vendor.\n- **ADM:** automated decision-making, decisions about people made or substantially shaped by software. Disclosure duties begin 10 December 2026."}, {"slug": "class-4-talking-to-the-software", "sourceFile": "04-class-4-talking-to-the-software.md", "title": "Class 4: Talking to the Software", "subtitle": "Prompting without the prompt-guru nonsense: context, system prompts, and being specific", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 20-minute setup exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** write a request in four parts that gets a usable first draft, explain why long conversations degrade, install a standing system prompt on your platform of choice, and fix the three common failure modes without starting over.\n\n## Kill the magic thinking first\n\nThere is an entire cottage industry selling \"prompt engineering\" secrets, and most of it is superstition. No magic words, no incantations, no need to say please. A model is pattern-matching over everything currently on its desk, so better output comes from better desk contents: clearer instructions, more relevant material, fewer distractions. Everything in this class follows from that sentence, which is good news, because it means the skill is learnable in an evening by anyone who can write a clear work email.\n\n## The anatomy of a good request: four parts\n\n1. **Role.** Who the tool is acting as: \"You are helping a plumbing business in Geelong.\"\n2. **Task.** What you want produced: \"draft a quote cover email\".\n3. **Material.** The real ingredients, pasted or attached: the job details, the prices, the customer's original request.\n4. **Constraints.** The limits: length, tone, format, what to avoid, and what to do when unsure.\n\nMost disappointing results are missing parts three and four. People ask for the output without supplying the ingredients or the limits, then blame the tool for guessing. Here is the difference in one pair.\n\n- Weak: \"Write a professional email about the delay.\"\n- Strong: \"Write a 100-word email to Karen at Bayside Dental apologising that the hot-water unit install moves from Thursday to Tuesday the 21st, offering a $150 discount on the labour, and confirming the new date. Warm, plain English, no corporate phrases.\"\n\nThe weak version leaves the model a thousand plausible directions and it picks one at random. The strong version removes 999 of them. Every constraint you add is an edit you do not have to make later.\n\n## The context window, properly explained\n\nThe model works on a desk of fixed size, measured in tokens (mouthfuls of text, from Class 1). Your system prompt, your pasted documents, the conversation so far and the reply all share that desk, which is called the context window. When the desk overflows, the oldest material falls off the edge, and the model literally stops being able to see the start of the conversation. Three practical consequences. Very long chats get dumber near the end, so start a new chat for a new task. A 300-page PDF may be answered from a fraction of its content, so ask pointed questions about specific sections. And \"remember what I told you at the start\" is not a reliable instruction in a long thread, which is what standing settings in the next section are for.\n\n## The system prompt: your standing briefing\n\nA system prompt (also called custom instructions, personal context or project instructions, depending on the product) is text placed before every one of your messages. It persists, so it is where the rules you are tired of repeating live: who your business is, who you write to, your tone, your formats, and your bright lines such as \"never invent prices or dates\". Think of it as the induction briefing you give a new employee on their first morning, except you give it once and it is never forgotten.\n\nDocument 1 in your pack contains three complete templates, for retail, professional services and trades businesses, plus the exact menu path to paste them into ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot and local tools. Installing one is the single highest-value twenty minutes in this course.\n\n## Paste, upload, or project: three ways to show it stuff\n\n- **Pasting into the chat** puts material on the desk immediately and in full, and it vanishes when the chat ends. Best for the one crucial document, right now.\n- **Uploading a file to the chat** converts your PDF, Word file, spreadsheet or photo into text and puts it on the desk for this task. Clean digital documents convert well. Tables, diagrams and photographed pages convert imperfectly.\n- **Adding files to a project, Gem, notebook or knowledge base** stores the material once and makes it available to every conversation in that space, with the product selecting the relevant slices per question. Best for standing reference such as policies, price lists and templates.\n\nDocument 2 covers the details, including the honest limits of what these tools can do with files. The rule of thumb: library for standing material, paste for today's authority.\n\n## Iteration is the actual skill\n\nThe first output is a draft, not a deliverable. The working rhythm is: ask, read critically, correct (\"that is too formal, and you invented a deadline, remove it\"), narrow, re-run. Two or three correction rounds reliably beat one perfect attempt, because you are steering a pattern-matcher with feedback rather than programming a machine. One phrase earns its place in your repertoire: \"Before answering, list what information you would need that I have not given you.\" It converts a guessing model into a question-asking one, and questions are cheaper than corrections.\n\n## Three failure modes and their fixes\n\n- **Vague and generic output.** You under-specified. Add constraints and paste real material.\n- **Wrong tone.** Fix it once in the system prompt, not message by message.\n- **Invented facts.** The model had a gap and filled it with plausible texture. Supply the facts, tell it to answer only from the material you provided and to say \"not in the material provided\" otherwise, and check every name, number, date and citation anyway. Grounding instructions reduce invention. They do not eliminate it, and checking stays non-negotiable.\n\n## The furniture around the engine\n\nModern products bolt useful features onto the model, and they differ by vendor because they are car features, not engine features. Live web search fixes the \"knows nothing recent\" problem, with the caveat that it reads the web imperfectly, like you do at speed. Editable output panes, called Canvas in ChatGPT and Artifacts in Claude, let you revise a document in place instead of regenerating whole answers. Copilot drafts inside Word and Outlook, and Gemini inside Gmail and Docs, which matters if your business already lives in those suites. For basic text tasks all four major products are close enough that your existing software ecosystem and your data-handling tier should decide, not benchmark scores.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"Prompting is a new profession and you need a course in magic words.\"** Prompt libraries full of \"act as a world-class expert with 20 years of experience\" are mostly theatre, and modern models respond to plain, specific instructions at least as well as to elaborate role-play. The mechanics that change output are unglamorous: put the relevant material in front of the model, state precisely what you want and what to avoid, correct it when it misses, and persist your standing instructions so you are not retyping them. A plumber who can describe a job accurately to an apprentice can prompt well, because the skill is the same one: knowing the job, specifying the standard, checking the work. What is genuinely worth learning is the furniture around the engine, projects, files, search, memory, and that is product-specific, which is why your document pack tells you where each button lives.\n\n## Your exercise: build your standing setup\n\n1. Draft a five-line system prompt for your own business using this skeleton: who we are, who we write to, tone rules, format rules, and one \"never\" rule such as \"never invent prices or dates\". Start from the matching template in Document 1.\n2. Paste it into the right place on your platform. Document 9 has the click-paths. If you use a work-managed tool, send the template to your admin instead and note that as done.\n3. Start a new chat and give the tool one real document plus one real task, such as \"summarise this into five bullets for a customer who has never seen it\". Nothing sensitive, or redact first.\n4. Run one correction round using the failure-mode fixes above. Note what changed.\n5. Write down the one recurring task this setup will handle next week. That sentence is the point of the exercise, because the productivity gain is not in one great prompt. It is in a standing setup that makes the tool useful on the tenth ordinary Tuesday.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- My four-part request skeleton is written somewhere I can copy it.\n- My system prompt is installed, or queued with my admin.\n- I know which failure mode I meet most often and which fix matches it.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Prompt:** what you type. **System prompt or custom instructions:** standing text the product places before your prompts to shape behaviour.\n- **Context window:** the model's desk. All the text it can consider at once, measured in tokens.\n- **Iteration:** correcting and re-running. The real skill of prompting.\n- **Grounding instruction:** telling the model to answer only from material you provided and to say so when the material runs out. Reduces invention, does not eliminate it.\n- **Project, Gem, notebook, GPT:** product features, names vary by vendor, that bundle standing instructions plus files into a reusable workspace.\n- **Canvas or Artifacts:** an editable document pane beside the chat, so you revise output in place.\n- **Retrieval:** when a product with many stored files selects the relevant slices to put on the desk for each prompt instead of loading everything.\n- **Live search:** a product feature that fetches current web pages for questions about recent events. Check anything that matters."}, {"slug": "class-5-software-without-the-software-bill", "sourceFile": "05-class-5-software-without-the-software-bill.md", "title": "Class 5: Software Without the Software Bill", "subtitle": "The collapsed cost of custom software, AI-assisted building, and what is realistic for a ten-person business", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 30-minute build-or-decide exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** explain why custom software used to cost six figures and now can cost an afternoon, sort any build idea into one of three tiers with honest boundaries, and run a four-week path from annoying process to working internal tool.\n\n## Why software was expensive, specifically\n\nIn 1995, building software meant developers billing by the month, servers you bought and housed, licences per user, and distribution on disks with manuals. Every extra copy and every extra user cost real money, so the industry's answer was shrink-wrapped software: build once, sell to thousands, customise for nobody small. Every small business learned to bend its processes around off-the-shelf tools, because bending the tools was unaffordable. Remember that shape, because the last thirty years have dismantled it piece by piece.\n\n## What collapsed, and in what order\n\nThree collapses stacked on top of each other. Infrastructure: cloud computing turned servers into an hourly rental, so running software for ten users costs roughly nothing. Distribution: the internet made copying free, which is why the marginal cost (the cost of one extra copy or one extra user) of software is now close to zero. And now, production: AI coding assistants (tools that write, explain and fix code from plain-language requests, such as GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Claude Code and the code modes inside ChatGPT, Gemini and Copilot) generate large amounts of routine code for you. The 1s and 0s were already free to copy and run at scale. The expensive human labour of writing them has become dramatically cheaper. That combination, the near-zero marginal cost of customised software, is the biggest shift happening right now, bigger than any robot story, and it is the whole of this class.\n\n## What that means for a ten-person business, concretely\n\nThese are real shapes, not fantasies. A quoting tool that turns your standard rates and job types into branded PDF quotes. A job-intake form that feeds a tidy spreadsheet and drafts the confirmation email. An internal search across ten years of PDFs, policies and supplier catalogues, which is also the best use case for a local model from Class 3. A booking page for your niche that no software vendor bothered to build. A script that renames, sorts and backs up the photos from every job. In 2010 each of these was a $50,000 to $200,000 conversation. Today each is an afternoon to a weekend with an AI assistant, plus, for anything customer-facing, a few hundred dollars of professional review and hosting.\n\n## A worked example, start to finish\n\nHere is what a small build looks like in practice, described as you would experience it. You open a chat assistant and describe the tool in plain English: \"Build me a one-page web tool where I enter a customer name, an invoice amount and days overdue, and it writes my reminder email in my usual tone. Internal use, fake data only.\" The assistant writes the code, roughly a hundred lines of a single file. You save it, open it in your browser, and it works. Ten minutes, start to finish, for the demo version.\n\nNow the honest part, which is the actual lesson. That demo has no database, no logins, no backups and no security review. It runs wherever you saved it. If it breaks, nobody is on call. Turning it into software the business relies on means adding hosting (the rented computer where a tool lives so people can reach it), authentication if anyone outside you touches it, backups, and someone who owns the day it fails at 4pm on a Friday. That gap between \"works on the demo\" and \"safe to rely on\" is where judgement lives, and the three tiers below are how you keep it honest.\n\n## The three tiers of build, and which one you are doing\n\n**Tier one, no build.** Configure tools you already pay for: a Copilot agent over your SharePoint, a Gemini Gem over your Drive, an automation with an AI step. Most \"we should build something\" needs die here, cheaply.\n\n**Tier two, assisted build.** Describe a small tool to an AI assistant, keep it internal or low-stakes, use fake data, accept that you cannot audit the code. This is where the napkin ideas live, and it is genuinely safe if the stakes stay low.\n\n**Tier three, real software.** Anything taking payments, holding customer data at scale, or that the business would hurt to lose. Tier three always needs a professional in the loop, even if AI wrote 90 percent of the code, because someone must own security, backups and support. A few hundred dollars of review is not an insult to the AI's work. It is insurance on yours.\n\n## \"Vibe coding\", named and bounded\n\nThe popular name for describing software in plain language and accepting the generated code without reading it is vibe coding. It works astonishingly well for prototypes (first working versions built to learn, not to rely on) and internal tools. It produces software the builder cannot audit, which is fine at tier two and dangerous at tier three. The rule this class teaches: vibe-code freely for internal, low-stakes tools, never for anything that holds other people's personal information, takes payments, or would hurt to lose. An AI-written app that works in the demo is not the same as an app that is secure, backed up and maintainable, and the difference only shows up under stress.\n\n## Cloud versus local for software work\n\nCloud AI coding tools give you the most capable engines, and your code, sometimes your whole codebase, goes to the vendor, so check the tier's training and retention settings exactly as you would for any other data. Business tiers generally exclude your code from training. Consumer tiers may not. Local LLM-assisted development (running a coding model on your own hardware through Ollama or LM Studio inside your editor) means your code never leaves the building, which matters for agencies under non-disclosure agreements, government-adjacent work, or firms with strict client confidentiality. The trade-off is capability: local coding models are decent for boilerplate and explanation and noticeably weaker on large refactors and hard bugs. The hybrid most small firms land on: local or business-tier tools for client code, consumer cloud tools for learning and throwaway scripts.\n\n## The cost reality check\n\nWhat it actually costs in 2026: an AI subscription at roughly $20 to $30 per person per month, or free tiers for light use. Hosting for a small internal tool, free to about $30 per month. A domain, $15 to $25 per year. Your time, or a junior's. And for tier three, a professional review from a few hundred to a few thousand dollars, which is still a rounding error against the 1995 numbers. Budget honestly for the line item everyone forgets: maintenance. Software is a plant, not a purchase. Dependencies (other people's free code libraries your tool leans on) need occasional updates, and nobody updates what nobody owns.\n\n## Buying versus building, the updated rule\n\nThe old rule was \"never build what you can buy.\" The new rule is: buy for anything standard, such as email, accounting and payroll; build for the 10 percent that is genuinely specific to how your business works; never build anything regulated, such as payments or health records, yourself. AI-assisted building moved the line. It did not erase it.\n\n## The traps, named so you can step over them\n\n- **Security defaults.** AI-generated apps often ship without authentication, input checking or proper handling of secrets (passwords, API keys and similar credentials that software needs and attackers want) unless you ask. Ask explicitly, and have a professional verify.\n- **The laptop problem.** \"It only runs on Brad's laptop\" is a business continuity incident waiting to happen. Host it or accept it is a prototype forever.\n- **Data leaks through the build process.** Pasting your real customer database into a consumer chatbot to help it build the tool is the Class 3 mistake in a new costume. Fake data in, real data later, in your own systems.\n- **The maintenance cliff.** The person who vibe-coded it leaves, and the business inherits software nobody can explain. Write a half-page note while you remember: what it does, where it lives, what breaks first.\n\n## A realistic adoption path, four weeks\n\nWeek one: pick one annoying internal process, the one everyone grumbles about. Week two: prototype it at tier two with fake data. Week three: use it daily and note every failure, because the failure list is the specification. Week four: decide. Keep it as-is, park it, or budget a professional review to productionise it (the professional work of making a prototype secure, backed up, monitored and supported). Total exposure before the decision: a subscription and some evenings. That path is reproducible for every idea on your napkin list.\n\n## What this does to the software you already pay for\n\nOne level-headed market note. When custom software is cheap, generic software that merely digitises a form faces pressure, and vendors respond by bolting on AI features, which is the \"powered by AI\" translation exercise from Class 2. You now have one strong question at renewal: what would this capability cost to build for our specific workflow? Sometimes the answer is still \"buy\". Now it is at least a real comparison instead of a shrug.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"Only programmers can build software, so non-technical owners should stay out of it.\"** That was true when building meant years of specialised labour, and it stopped being true in stages, with AI-assisted coding removing most of what remained. A non-technical owner who can describe their process precisely, someone who knows exactly what happens between a customer's phone call and the job sheet, can now produce working internal tools with a chat assistant without reading a line of the code it writes. The myth survives because it is commercially useful to vendors, and because the caveats are real and easy to conflate with \"you can't\". The honest statement is narrower and more useful: you can build internal, low-stakes tools yourself this month, and you should not build anything that holds other people's personal information, takes payments, or that the business would suffer to lose, without a professional owning security and support. Inside that boundary, the person closest to the problem is often the best person to build the fix.\n\n## Your exercise: napkin to prototype\n\n1. Write the single most annoying repetitive process in your work on one card, in three sentences, the way you would explain it to a new employee. Reject your first draft if it is vague.\n2. Convert the card into a build request using this template: what the tool takes in, what it does, what it produces, who uses it, plus the line \"this is an internal prototype, use fake data only\".\n3. Run it in any assistant with code ability, save the result as a single HTML file if offered, and open it in your browser. If something breaks, paste the error back in and ask for a fix. That loop is the whole skill.\n4. Use the tool once on a realistic but fake example and write down the first thing you would fix.\n5. Make the tier call in one sentence: usable internally next week as-is, or needs a professional before anyone outside the business touches it? The gap between those two lists is the lesson of this class.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can explain the marginal cost collapse in one sentence to a colleague.\n- I have one napkin idea sorted into a tier, with reasons.\n- I know which traps my idea would walk into, and the first step over each.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Marginal cost:** what one extra copy or one extra user costs. For software in 2026, near zero. The whole story of this class.\n- **AI coding assistant:** a tool that writes, explains and fixes code from plain-language requests.\n- **Vibe coding:** describing software conversationally and accepting the generated code without reading it. Fine for prototypes and internal tools, never for sensitive or customer-facing work.\n- **Prototype:** a first working version, built to learn, not to rely on.\n- **Hosting:** the rented computer where a tool lives so people can reach it.\n- **Dependencies:** other people's free code libraries your tool leans on, which need occasional updates.\n- **Secrets:** passwords, API keys and similar credentials. Store them specially, never paste them into chat tools.\n- **Productionise:** the professional work of turning a prototype into software that is secure, backed up, monitored and supported.\n- **Local LLM-assisted development:** running a coding model on your own hardware inside your editor, so your code never leaves the building."}, {"slug": "class-6-keeping-your-house-in-order", "sourceFile": "06-class-6-keeping-your-house-in-order.md", "title": "Class 6: Keeping Your House in Order", "subtitle": "Shadow AI, data security basics, and the paperwork that protects a small business", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 30-minute rollout draft.\n**By the end of this class you can:** explain shadow AI without villainising anyone, run a one-week governance rollout for a business up to about fifty people, answer \"did you use AI on my file?\" honestly and calmly, and name the single role that makes all of it stick.\n\n## Start with the uncomfortable truth\n\nYour staff are already using AI. Every workplace survey since 2023 has found the same shape: usage runs well ahead of policy, everywhere, including in businesses that believe they have banned it. The only open question is whether you know where the usage happens, with what data, and what occurs when something goes wrong. This class closes that gap in one week, without blame, because blame is what keeps the next mistake hidden.\n\n## Shadow AI, defined plainly\n\nShadow AI is staff using AI tools for work without the business knowing or approving: pasting the client list into a free chatbot to tidy it up, drafting the tender response on a personal account at the kitchen table, summarising a roster that contains everyone's home addresses in a phone app. It is called shadow because the usage is invisible to the business, not because the staff are villains. Usually they are being enterprising with the tools they have, which is a trait you otherwise want.\n\n## Why it is a genuine risk, in three lines\n\nData risk: consumer-tier tools may retain content, may use it to improve models unless a toggle says otherwise, sit on overseas servers, and come with no contract you can point to. Accuracy risk: unverified output reaches customers, as the Deloitte and Air Canada cases in Class 2 showed at scale. Compliance risk: under the Privacy Act you remain responsible for personal information your business collects, including when an employee sends it to a US chatbot from their phone, which is the APP 8 and APP 11 point from Class 3.\n\n## Why bans fail, and what works instead\n\nBanning AI usage in 2026 is like banning the photocopier in 1986: unenforceable, invisible, and it guarantees the usage that does happen is hidden, which is exactly the risk you are trying to reduce. What works is the amnesty-plus-tools pattern. Approve two or three tools on business tiers. Publish a one-page policy saying what may and may not be pasted where. Add a no-blame reporting line for oops moments. Then ask teams what they are already using, with no consequences for honesty, so the useful habits get formalised and the risky ones get moved to the right tool. Document 3 is exactly this policy, written to be handed out rather than filed.\n\n## The four habits that prevent almost every incident\n\n1. **Tier discipline.** Work data only on work accounts, on business tiers or local tools with contractual protections. Check which account you are signed into before pasting, every time, because the icons look alike.\n2. **The decision drill.** Personal information, secrets, or anything under NDA? If yes, change tool or redact. From Class 3, repeated here because it does most of the work.\n3. **Human check on everything that leaves the building.** Names, numbers, dates, quotes, citations. The check is usually faster than doing the task from scratch, which is why the honest productivity claim is \"faster with review\".\n4. **Same-day reporting.** Anything sensitive that went where it should not: tell the policy owner the same day, no drama. Fast reporting turns incidents into log entries. Delay is the only thing that turns them into crises.\n\n## The one-page policy, walked through\n\nOpen Document 3 and read it once properly. It covers where the business stands (we want you using these tools well, not secretly), the approved tool list, what may be pasted, what must never be pasted, the checking rule, the same-day oops procedure, and honest disclosure to clients. Your job this week is to make it yours: replace the bracketed business name and contact person, cross out anything that does not apply, and add one industry-specific prohibition. A medical centre adds \"no patient identifiers, ever\". A law firm adds \"nothing covered by client privilege without the principal's approval\". One page, one read, one signature line. Anything longer does not get read, which is why it is one page.\n\n## The incident card, walked through\n\nDocument 10 covers the four scenarios that actually occur, each as a short calm checklist: an employee realises they pasted sensitive data into a consumer tool; a client asks whether AI was used on their deliverable; a vendor breach hits the news; wrong AI output went to a customer. Read all four once before you need them, then pin the card where people can see it. Rehearse one out loud, really, out loud, because the scenario worth practising is the client question. The honest answer, prepared in advance, is: yes where it was used, here is what a human checked, here is our policy. Delivered calmly, that answer builds trust. Improvised under pressure, the same facts sound like a confession.\n\n## Vendor governance for the rest of us\n\nYou will not run enterprise procurement, and you do not need to. Three habits carry most of the value. Keep a one-line register of every AI tool in use: name, tier, who uses it, what data touches it. Before any purchase, run the Class 2 vendor questions and get the business-tier terms and data processing agreement in writing. Review the register quarterly, in the same calendar slot as the pricing and privacy-settings review from Documents 5 and 6, because tiers, prices and policies all move.\n\n## Disclosure: when to say AI was involved\n\nThere is no blanket Australian legal requirement to label ordinary business documents as AI-assisted. Specific duties are tightening anyway, so work from principles. Disclose on request, honestly and without theatre. Disclose proactively where the work is advice: legal, accounting, medical, engineering, or anything safety-related. Follow any contract that restricts AI use, because breaching one is a commercial and legal problem regardless of AI law, as Deloitte discovered. And never imply a human verified work that nobody checked, because misleading conduct rules apply and, more importantly, being caught ends relationships that honest disclosure would have strengthened.\n\n## Accountability without a compliance department\n\nAssign one named person to own the policy, the tool register, the quarterly review and the incident log. In a ten-person business that is often the owner or the office manager. Give them the document pack and half an hour a month. Ownership is the difference between paperwork and practice. Every failed rollout in every industry comes down to nobody owning the thing, and every successful one to somebody doing exactly this.\n\n## The regulatory horizon, briefly\n\nAustralian reform is staged, and the direction of travel is clear even where the dates are not. The first tranche of Privacy Act amendments is in force, with stronger regulator powers, higher penalties and a statutory tort for serious invasions of privacy since June 2025. The automated decision-making transparency duty arrives on 10 December 2026. Further tranches, including the fate of the small-business exemption, remain in the policy pipeline. The sensible posture is to run your business as if the exemption will not save you, because it is shrinking and because good practice here is cheap: one page, one owner, one week.\n\n## The one-week rollout plan\n\n- **Day one:** pick your named owner and your two or three approved tools.\n- **Day two:** circulate Document 3 with a covering note that says, in your words, \"we want you using these well, not secretly\".\n- **Day three:** collect the amnesty answers. What is already in use, no consequences. Formalise the useful parts.\n- **Day four:** move approved tools to business tiers, or set up the local option. Apply the opt-out settings from Document 5 to anything staying on consumer tiers in the meantime.\n- **Day five:** pin Document 10 where people can see it and run a ten-minute toolbox talk (a short team briefing, usually at the start of a shift) using Document 8, the plain-English FAQ.\n\nThat is a complete governance rollout for a business of up to about fifty people, done in a week, at the cost of a subscription or two. There is no version of this that requires a consultant, a project board or a quarter.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"We are too small to be a target, so AI data risks do not apply to us.\"** Attackers and data brokers do not sort victims by headcount. They sort by weakness, and a small business with staff pasting client data into free consumer chatbots, no written policy, no tool register and no incident plan is weak in exactly the measurable ways. Human error and compromised credentials account for the bulk of real incidents, well ahead of Hollywood-grade hacking, and a chatbot account on a personal email with a reused password sits squarely in that category. \"Too small\" also fails as a legal defence: the Privacy Act's core obligations already attach to many small organisations regardless of turnover, the statutory tort has no turnover test, and your clients increasingly flow obligations down through contracts, where \"we are too small for that\" loses to a competitor holding this course's one-page policy. The correct conclusion is not fear. It is that the controls a small business needs are proportionately small: one page, one owner, one week, all of which this class hands you.\n\n## Your exercise: draft your rollout, tonight\n\n1. Fill in the rollout worksheet in this course: named owner, approved tools and their tiers, first line of the tool register, reporting contact for oops moments, date of next week's toolbox talk.\n2. Mark up Document 3 with your specifics: business name, contact person, your one industry-specific prohibition, and cross out anything that does not apply.\n3. Choose one incident scenario from Document 10 and read its checklist out loud to someone, then let them ask one \"but what if\" question and answer it without looking. Swap and repeat with a second scenario.\n4. Write the first sentence you will say to your team on Monday. Say it out loud once. If it sounds like a police announcement, rewrite it until it sounds like an invitation, because the amnesty only works if people believe it.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can define shadow AI without blaming anyone.\n- My named owner, approved tools and reporting line are written down.\n- I have rehearsed the client-question answer out loud at least once.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Shadow AI:** AI tools used for work without the business knowing or approving. Invisible usage, not malicious usage.\n- **AI use policy:** the one page telling staff which tools are approved, what may be pasted, what must be checked, and who to tell. Document 3.\n- **Tool register:** a one-line-per-tool list: name, tier, who uses it, what data touches it, when last reviewed.\n- **Amnesty:** a no-consequences window for staff to declare current AI usage so it can be formalised or corrected. The single most effective shadow-AI tool.\n- **Incident:** sensitive data pasted where it should not have been, wrong output reaching a customer, or a vendor breach affecting you. Document 10 turns each into a checklist.\n- **Owner:** the one named person accountable for the policy, register, reviews and incident log. Without this role, everything else is decoration.\n- **Disclosure:** telling a client, customer or reader that AI was involved in producing work. Required by some contracts, and a trust-builder in most other situations.\n- **Flow-down obligations:** requirements your clients or funders put in contracts that become your requirements. Increasingly include AI governance.\n- **OAIC:** the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner, the privacy regulator, whose generative AI guidance is the plainest official statement of what reasonable steps look like.\n- **Toolbox talk:** a short team briefing, five to fifteen minutes, usually at the start of a shift. The right format for rolling out Documents 3, 8 and 10."}], "docs": [{"slug": "doc-01-system-prompt-starter-kit", "sourceFile": "07-doc-01-system-prompt-starter-kit.md", "title": "Document 1: System Prompt Starter Kit", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini and Gems (Google), Copilot (Microsoft), and local tools such as Ollama and LM Studio running Meta's Llama or similar open models.\n**Version:** October 2026. Interfaces change; the concepts do not.\n\n---\n\n## What a system prompt is\n\nA system prompt is a block of standing instructions that sits in front of everything you type. You write the message; the system prompt is written once and applies to every message. The model treats it as higher-priority guidance than your individual requests, which is why it is the right place for rules you are tired of repeating: who your business is, how you want things written, what the tool must never do.\n\nThink of it as the induction briefing you give a new employee on their first morning, except you give it once and the employee never forgets it, and never takes a sickie.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nWithout one, every chat starts from zero. The tool guesses your tone, guesses your audience, guesses your conventions, and you spend your first three messages correcting it. With a good system prompt, the first draft lands much closer to usable. It is the cheapest, fastest quality upgrade available in any of these products, and almost nobody uses it properly.\n\nA system prompt cannot make the model more knowledgeable or stop it inventing facts. It shapes behaviour, tone, format and priorities. You still check the output.\n\n## How the same idea is named on each platform\n\n| Platform | What the feature is called | Where it lives (as at October 2026) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ChatGPT | Custom instructions, plus per-Project instructions | Settings, then Personalisation, then Custom instructions. Two boxes: \"What would you like ChatGPT to know about you?\" and \"How would you like ChatGPT to respond?\". Separately: any Project you create has its own instructions box that applies only inside that Project. |\n| Claude | Profile / personal instructions, plus per-Project custom instructions | Settings, then Profile, \"What personal preferences should Claude consider in responses?\" Projects: open the Project, then the instructions field in Project settings. |\n| Gemini | Personal context, plus Gems (custom assistants) | Settings, then Personal context (a single \"what should Gemini know\" box). Gems: create a Gem and paste your system prompt into its instructions; that Gem then behaves that way every time you open it. |\n| Copilot (consumer) | Personalisation / preferred responses | In the Copilot app or copilot.microsoft.com: Settings, then Personalisation. |\n| Microsoft 365 Copilot (work account) | Usually set by your IT admin; users get limited personal preferences | Admins can configure organisational prompts and agents in the Microsoft 365 admin centre and Copilot Studio. Individual staff typically cannot override them, which is the point. |\n| Workspace (Gemini in Gmail/Docs) | No user system prompt inside Gmail/Docs drafting; use Gems for standing behaviour | Create a Gem with your template, or paste your instructions at the top of a \"Help me write\" prompt. |\n| Ollama / LM Studio (local models) | System prompt field in the chat settings | LM Studio: the chat screen has a system prompt box on the right-hand settings panel. Ollama: pass a system prompt when you run the model, or use any front-end app that exposes the field. |\n\n**Platform differences worth knowing.** ChatGPT's global custom instructions apply everywhere, which is convenient and occasionally annoying when you want work mode and personal mode separated; use Projects for work. Claude's Projects are the strongest way to keep separate business contexts (one Project per client type, each with its own instructions and files). Gems are the same idea in Google's world and live inside your Google account. Microsoft 365 Copilot deliberately takes standing instructions away from individuals and gives them to the organisation's admin, which is good governance and occasionally frustrating.\n\n---\n\n## Template 1: Small retail business\n\nPaste into your platform's system prompt / custom instructions / Gem instructions field. Edit the square brackets.\n\n```\nYou assist [Business Name], an independent [retail type, e.g. hardware store / boutique / gift shop] in [town/suburb], with a team of [number]. We sell [main product categories] to [customer types, e.g. local tradies, gift buyers, tourists].\n\nWhen writing for us:\n- Plain Australian English. Warm but not gushy. Short sentences.\n- Customers come first: product names, prices and promotions must match exactly what I give you. Never invent prices, stock levels, dates or product claims.\n- If I ask for social media copy: [platform, e.g. Facebook and Instagram], maximum [number] sentences, one clear call to action, no hashtags unless I ask.\n- If I ask for customer emails: friendly, brief, answer the customer's actual problem, sign off as [name/role].\n- For supplier or landlord correspondence: polite, factual, firm, no slang.\n- Never promise refunds, exchanges, warranties or delivery dates unless I have given you the policy in the message. If the answer depends on our policy and I have not supplied it, ask me.\n- When you are unsure, say so plainly instead of guessing.\n- Do not use the words \"delve\", \"elevate\", \"game-changing\" or \"unlock\".\n```\n\n## Template 2: Professional services firm (accounting, legal, consulting)\n\n```\nYou assist [Firm Name], a [discipline] firm in [city] with [number] staff. Our clients are [client types, e.g. small businesses, individuals, SMEs in the construction sector]. Our tone is [e.g. precise and reassuring].\n\nRules:\n- Plain Australian English, professional register. Define any technical term the first time you use it.\n- You are a drafting assistant, not an adviser. You do not provide accounting, legal or financial advice, and you must never state a rule of law, a tax rate, a threshold or a deadline as fact unless I supplied it in the conversation. Where a claim needs verification, mark it [VERIFY] in the output.\n- Structure documents clearly: headings, short paragraphs, numbered lists for steps.\n- When summarising material I provide, distinguish clearly between what the document says and what you infer.\n- Flag, in a separate line at the end, anything in my material that looks time-sensitive, jurisdiction-specific, or beyond your knowledge, so a qualified human reviews it before it goes to a client.\n- Confidentiality: I will only paste client material that our firm's AI policy permits. Never assume context I have not given.\n- Output defaults: emails under 150 words; memos with a three-sentence executive summary up top; tables where data is involved.\n```\n\n## Template 3: Trades or field-services business\n\n```\nYou assist [Business Name], a [trade, e.g. plumbing / electrical / landscaping] business based in [region], with [number] on the tools and [number] in the office. Our customers are [e.g. homeowners, builders, property managers] across [service area].\n\nWhen writing for us:\n- Straight-talking Australian English. No corporate fluff, no jargon the customer would not use. Read it out loud; if a tradie would not say it, rewrite it.\n- Quotes and job descriptions: use only the figures, dates and scope I give you. Never estimate prices, materials or timeframes yourself. If something is missing, list what you need before drafting.\n- Customer emails and texts: short, friendly, confirm the facts (what, when, how much), and always include what happens next.\n- Job sheet and work-completion summaries: bullet points, plain language a homeowner understands, note anything the customer should watch for.\n- Supplier and subcontractor messages: direct, factual, no apologies we do not owe.\n- Compliance language (certificates, standards, permits): never paraphrase a requirement from memory. Use only what I paste in, and mark anything else [CHECK WITH LICENSED PERSON].\n- If I dictate rough notes, turn them into clean text without adding facts.\n```\n\n---\n\n## How to install your template, step by step\n\n**ChatGPT.** Open Settings (your profile picture or name, bottom left on web; top right on mobile). Choose Personalisation, then Custom instructions. Paste into the second box (\"How would you like ChatGPT to respond?\"), and put one or two lines of business identity into the first box. Save. For a dedicated work space, create a Project (sidebar, plus button), open its settings, and paste the full template into Project instructions; files added to that Project stay available to every chat inside it. Note: on consumer plans, check Settings, Data controls, and turn off model training if you have not already.\n\n**Claude.** Open Settings, then Profile. Paste your template into the personal preferences field. For per-context use, create a Project (sidebar), open Project settings, and paste the template into the Project's custom instructions; add reference files to the Project knowledge as needed.\n\n**Gemini.** Two options. Quick: Settings, Personal context, paste a condensed version (the field is shorter than a full template, so keep the identity line and the top five rules). Better for business: go to Gems (sidebar or gemini.google.com), create a Gem, give it a name like \"[Business] assistant\", paste the full template as its instructions, and use that Gem for work tasks. Workspace users: the same Gems are available inside Google apps depending on your plan.\n\n**Copilot (consumer).** In the Copilot app or website, open Settings, then Personalisation, and paste what the field accepts. **Microsoft 365 Copilot (work):** individual staff usually cannot set an organisational system prompt; send your template to whoever administers Microsoft 365 and ask for it to be configured centrally (Copilot Studio or admin prompt settings). That is a feature: one version of the truth for the whole team.\n\n**Local (Ollama / LM Studio).** In LM Studio's chat panel, expand the settings on the right and paste the template into the System prompt field; it applies to that chat and can be saved with the preset. In Ollama itself, the system prompt is set per session or in a Modelfile; most people use a front-end app that gives you the box. Advantage: your instructions and everything else stay on your machine.\n\n## Writing your own: the five-part formula\n\nEvery template above has the same skeleton, and yours should too: (1) identity, who we are, in one sentence; (2) audience, who we write to; (3) tone, three or four concrete rules, not adjectives like \"professional\"; (4) formats, your default lengths and structures per task type; (5) never rules, the bright lines: no invented prices, no advice beyond your licence, no compliance language from memory, say \"I'm not sure\" instead of guessing. Keep it under about 250 words; a system prompt is a briefing, not a novel, and every token of it sits on the model's desk in every conversation.\n\n## Testing it\n\nAfter installing, run the same three test prompts you would have run before: draft a customer email, summarise a document, write a social post. Compare against your memory of the old defaults. Then break it deliberately: ask for something the never-rules forbid (\"quote a price for this job\") and confirm it asks you for the figures instead of inventing them. If it invents them, strengthen that line in the template. This ten-minute test is worth more than any prompt-engineering course."}, {"slug": "doc-02-data-ingestion-guide", "sourceFile": "08-doc-02-data-ingestion-guide.md", "title": "Document 2: How to Show Your AI Stuff (Data Ingestion Guide)", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini and NotebookLM (Google), Copilot and Microsoft 365 Copilot (Microsoft), and local tools (Ollama, LM Studio) with any chat front-end that accepts files.\n**Version:** October 2026.\n\n---\n\n## The one idea behind all of this\n\nA model only knows what is on its desk right now. \"Showing your AI stuff\" means putting the right material on that desk: your price list, the client's email, last year's report, the policy document. Every feature in this guide (paste, upload, projects, connectors, knowledge bases) is just a different way of getting material onto the desk, with different lifespans and different reach.\n\nBefore you show it anything, apply the decision drill from Class 3: does this material contain personal information, secrets, or anything under NDA? If yes, use a business-tier tool, a contracted Microsoft/Google ecosystem tool, or a local tool. Consumer free tiers are for material you would not mind a stranger reading, because you cannot fully un-send it.\n\n## The four ways to give a tool context\n\n**1. Pasting into the chat.** The oldest and most reliable method. Copy the text, paste it, done. It is on the desk immediately, in full, exactly as you gave it. Downsides: it vanishes when the chat ends, very long pastes eat the desk (the context window) and can push earlier conversation off the edge, and formatting (tables especially) can get mangled. Best for: one-off material under a few pages.\n\n**2. Uploading a file to the chat.** The paperclip or plus button. The product converts your file to text (and for images, describes or reads them) and puts it on the desk. Good for PDFs, Word files, spreadsheets, and photos of documents. Know what happens underneath: text is extracted, which is near-perfect for clean digital documents, lossy for tables and diagrams, and dependent on OCR (optical character recognition, software that reads pictures of text) for scans and photos, which can misread. Very large files may be processed in slices rather than all at once, so \"read this 300-page PDF and tell me everything\" can quietly become \"read part of it.\" Ask pointed questions about specific sections and you get much better results.\n\n**3. Adding to a Project, Gem, Notebook or knowledge base.** The persistent option. ChatGPT Projects, Claude Projects, Google Gems and NotebookLM, and SharePoint-backed Microsoft 365 Copilot all let you attach files and instructions once, and every conversation in that space can draw on them. Under the hood, most products do not keep the whole library on the desk at all times; they search it and place the relevant slices per question (retrieval). Practical consequences: excellent for a standing body of material (your policies, your product catalogue, your templates); slightly less literal than a direct paste, so for the one document that matters most in this moment, also paste or attach it directly to the chat and say \"use the attached version as the authority.\"\n\n**4. Connecting live data sources.** The products increasingly plug into your world: Microsoft 365 Copilot reads your Outlook, Teams, Word, Excel and SharePoint content (respecting the permissions already set, which is its big advantage); Gemini can work across Gmail, Drive, Docs and Calendar for Workspace accounts; ChatGPT and Claude offer connectors and integrations for Drive, Gmail, SharePoint and common business apps on some tiers. Powerful and convenient, and it widens the data question from \"what did I paste\" to \"what can the tool reach.\" Turn on only the connections you actually use, prefer work accounts with admin controls, and remember the connector inherits your permissions, so anything you can see, it can see, including that spreadsheet you forgot you had access to.\n\n## \"I pasted it into the chat\" versus \"I uploaded it to a project\": the honest comparison\n\n| | Pasted into a chat | Uploaded to this chat | In a Project / Gem / Notebook | Live connector |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lifespan | This conversation only | This conversation only | The workspace, across conversations | Ongoing, live |\n| Fidelity | Exactly what you gave | Text-extracted; tables and scans can degrade | Extracted plus retrieval; may quote indirectly | Depends on the app |\n| Best for | The one crucial document, right now | PDFs, photos, spreadsheets for a task | Standing reference: policies, catalogues, templates | Working where your data already lives |\n| Risk to manage | Chat history retention | Same, plus file stored in vendor systems | Everything in the space is reachable by everyone in the space (shared projects especially) | Scope creep: it can reach more than you think |\n\nThe common beginner mistake is trusting a big library connection when a small precise paste would do, and the common advanced mistake is pasting the same 20-page document into every new chat instead of putting it in a Project once. Use the library for standing material and the paste for today's authority.\n\n## What the tools can and cannot actually do with your files\n\n**Can:** extract and read text from documents; read tables from spreadsheets into a workable form (imperfectly); read text in images via OCR; answer questions grounded in what you gave them; summarise, compare and restructure your material; in the coding products, work across whole codebases.\n\n**Cannot (reliably, in 2026):** keep perfect track of every number across a huge spreadsheet (they are pattern-matchers, not calculators; for arithmetic, ask them to write a formula or code, then check it); preserve the exact visual layout of your documents; understand diagrams and floor plans the way a human in your trade does; know which of two conflicting uploaded documents is current unless you tell it; remember anything across chats except through the persistence features above (and \"memory\" features store notes about you as text, which is Document 9's subject); guarantee nothing from your file was looked at by vendor systems for safety or abuse review under their policies, which is why tier choice matters more than any setting inside the chat.\n\n## File formats that work well, and formats that fight back\n\nWork well: plain text, Markdown, clean Word documents, PDFs generated from software (not photographed), CSV exports from spreadsheets, screenshots with clear text. Fight back: multi-column PDFs (text extraction can interleave columns), scanned documents at low quality, complex Excel files with merged cells, formulas and multiple sheets (upload a CSV export of just the data you need instead), PowerPoint decks heavy on graphics, and anything password-protected. When a file fights back, copy the relevant text out and paste it. Boring, effective, always works.\n\n## The pre-session checklist\n\nFive minutes before a working session with an AI tool. Print it, live by it.\n\n1. **Objective in one sentence.** What do I want to walk away with? (\"A draft quote email,\" not \"some help with the Johnson job.\")\n2. **Gather the material.** The documents, numbers, emails and policies the task actually needs. If the tool has to guess, the tool will invent.\n3. **Sensitivity screen.** Personal information? Secrets? NDA? If yes: business tier, Workspace/Microsoft ecosystem, or local tool. If unsure: redact first (names to initials, prices to placeholders you swap back later).\n4. **Pick the right persistence.** One-off task: paste or attach to a fresh chat. Recurring task: check the right Project/Gem exists and has current files in it. Delete or replace stale files in that space; old price lists in a knowledge base produce confidently wrong quotes.\n5. **Write the prompt in four parts.** Role, task, material, constraints (length, tone, format, what to avoid, what to do when unsure). Document 4 has the patterns.\n6. **Set the check standard.** Decide now what you will verify in the output: every name, number, date, price and quoted policy. Write \"[VERIFY]\" instructions into the prompt if it helps.\n7. **Fresh chat for fresh tasks.** Long threads degrade as the desk overflows. Start a new chat, let the Project carry the context.\n\n## After the session\n\nSave the useful output somewhere you own (your documents, not the chat history, which is not a filing system). If the session revealed a gap in your standing material (the tool needed your refund policy and no Project had it), add that file to the Project now. If anything sensitive was pasted by mistake, follow Document 10's first checklist the same day."}, {"slug": "doc-03-shadow-ai-policy", "sourceFile": "09-doc-03-shadow-ai-policy.md", "title": "Document 3: AI Use Policy (Plain English)", "subtitle": "", "md": "**[Business Name]. For every employee, contractor and volunteer.**\n**Owner of this policy: [Name, role]. Questions and reports go to: [email / phone].**\n**Version [X], [date]. Reviewed quarterly.**\n\n*Hand this to every person who works here. Read it once properly, keep it somewhere findable. It is two pages because anything longer does not get read.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Where we stand\n\nWe want you using AI tools. They save time, they improve drafts, and pretending they do not exist would be like banning email in 1998. What we cannot do is have that usage happening invisibly, with client data, on free personal accounts, with nobody able to answer for it. This policy exists to bring AI use into the open, not to ban it. Using AI at work is not a disciplinary matter. Hiding a mistake is harder to forgive than making one.\n\n## 2. What \"shadow AI\" means\n\nShadow AI is using AI tools for work without the business knowing: your personal ChatGPT account on your phone, a free chatbot a friend recommended, an AI feature buried in some app you installed. The risk is not that you are doing something wrong by being resourceful. The risks are that free consumer tools may keep your data, may use it to improve their models unless settings say otherwise, sit on overseas servers with no contract protecting us, and produce output nobody checked that ends up in front of a customer.\n\n**If you are already using something we have not approved: tell [Name]. There is no penalty for declaring it. We would much rather formalise a useful habit than discover it in an incident.**\n\n## 3. Approved tools\n\nThese are the tools we approve and pay for, on business accounts with contractual data protections:\n\n- [Tool 1, e.g. Microsoft 365 Copilot (sign in with your work account only)]\n- [Tool 2, e.g. ChatGPT Business (accounts issued by us; do not use your personal account for work)]\n- [Tool 3, e.g. our local/internal assistant (runs in the office; nothing leaves the building)]\n\nAnything not on this list needs a two-minute conversation with [Name] first. The list is reviewed quarterly.\n\n## 4. What you may put into an approved AI tool\n\n- General drafting: emails, social posts, letters, meeting summaries, job descriptions.\n- Material that is already public, or that contains no personal information and no secrets.\n- Business documents that the tool's account tier covers (everything on the approved list is on a tier where our data is not used to train the vendor's models).\n- Client material, only if: the tool is on the approved list, you paste the minimum needed, and you follow any client-specific restriction (some contracts restrict AI use; [Name] keeps the list).\n\n## 5. What you must never put into any AI tool\n\n- **Passwords, keys, logins, payment card numbers.** Never, into anything, ever.\n- **Full client lists, or personal information in bulk** (names with contact details, dates of birth, Medicare or tax file numbers, driver's licence numbers) unless [Name] has approved that exact use on a contracted tool.\n- **Health or medical information about any person**, unless we are a health provider and [Name] has approved a compliant tool in writing.\n- **Anything under a non-disclosure agreement or client confidentiality** unless the specific NDA permits it or [Name] says so.\n- **Employee grievances, disciplinary matters, payroll detail:** on approved tools only, minimised, and tell [Name] you are doing it.\n- **Financials that are not yet public** (pre-announcement results, tender pricing): on approved tools only.\n\nWhen in doubt, redact: \"Client A in Toowoomba\" instead of the name; \"[amount]\" instead of the figure. You can swap the real details back into the draft afterwards, in your own software.\n\n## 6. Checking the output: the non-negotiable part\n\nAI tools write fluently and are wrong with total confidence. They invent prices, dates, rules, references and quotes. So:\n\n- **You remain responsible for anything you send, file or publish**, whether a human or a machine drafted it. \"The AI said so\" is not a defence, with us or with a court.\n- Check every name, number, date, price, deadline and quoted rule against a source you trust before it leaves this business.\n- Anything going to a client, a regulator, or the public gets a second pair of eyes: yours plus the usual approver.\n- Never present AI output as verified professional advice (legal, accounting, medical, engineering) unless a qualified person here has actually verified it.\n\n## 7. If you have already pasted something sensitive: same-day procedure, no drama\n\n1. **Stop and note what happened.** Which tool, which account, roughly what data, what time.\n2. **Tell [Name] the same day.** Same-day reporting is always fine. Discoveries are what cause problems.\n3. **Delete the chat/conversation** in the tool, and if the tool has an \"exclude from training\" or temporary-chat setting, check it was on (it should have been; report if not).\n4. **[Name] assesses it** against our privacy obligations: was it personal information? Could it cause serious harm? Is it notifiable? Usually the answer is \"contained, logged, move on.\" Sometimes there is more to do, and doing it fast is what makes it small.\n5. **We log it** in the incident register, one line, no names attached to blame. The log exists to fix patterns, not people.\n\n## 8. Clients, honesty, and disclosure\n\nIf a client asks whether AI was used on their work, the answer is honest and unembarrassed: \"We use AI tools for drafting and summarising, on contracted business platforms, and a qualified person checks everything before it reaches you. Here is our policy.\" If a client's contract restricts AI use, we follow the contract. We never imply a human did work that a machine did unsupervised, and we never claim checks we did not perform.\n\n## 9. Personal use on work devices\n\nUsing AI tools for your own stuff on your own time and your own account is your business, with two limits: not on systems holding client data, and never pasting work material into personal accounts. The line is the data, not the device.\n\n## 10. Who to talk to\n\n- **Tool questions, approvals, new-tool requests:** [Name], [contact].\n- **\"I think I pasted something I shouldn't have\":** [Name], same day, any hour, no penalty for reporting.\n- **Client contract questions about AI:** [Name] before you start the work, not after.\n- **This policy is reviewed quarterly** against the tools we use and the rules that apply to us. Suggestions genuinely welcome; the best policies are the ones staff improve.\n\n---\n\n*Acknowledgement: I have read and understood this policy. Name: ______ Signed: ______ Date: ______*"}, {"slug": "doc-04-prompting-cheat-sheet", "sourceFile": "10-doc-04-prompting-cheat-sheet.md", "title": "Document 4: Prompting Cheat Sheet for Daily Tasks", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack. One page, pin it up.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, and any local model chat (Ollama, LM Studio).\n**Version:** October 2026.\n\n**The universal pattern:** Role + Task + Material + Constraints. Give it the ingredients and the limits, and the pattern-matcher has less room to invent.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Drafting emails\n\n**Pattern A (the situation):**\n> Draft an email to [name] at [business]. Situation: [facts, dates, amounts]. Goal: [what you want to happen]. Tone: [friendly/firm]. Max [X] words. Don't apologise unless the facts warrant it. End with a clear next step.\n\n**Pattern B (the reply):**\n> Here is the email I received: [paste]. Draft a reply that: [answers their question / declines the request / buys time until Friday]. Keep my usual tone: [brief, warm, Australian]. No corporate phrases. Max 100 words.\n\n*Platform notes:* All four major platforms do this well. Copilot inside Outlook drafts in place with your thread context, which is fastest if you are a Microsoft shop; Gemini inside Gmail does the same for Google shops. ChatGPT and Claude are best when you paste the thread in and want tighter control of tone.\n\n## 2. Summarising meeting notes\n\n**Pattern A (rough to readable):**\n> These are my rough notes from [meeting, date, attendees]: [paste]. Rewrite them as clean minutes: decisions made, action items with owner and due date, and open questions. Keep it under one page. Do not add anything I did not write. If an owner or date is missing, mark it [UNCLEAR].\n\n**Pattern B (the long meeting):**\n> Here is the transcript/notes from a [X]-minute meeting: [paste or attach]. Give me: (1) a five-sentence summary, (2) every decision, (3) every action item as \"who / what / by when\", (4) anything that was raised but parked. Quote exact figures only where they appear in the notes.\n\n*Platform notes:* Claude and ChatGPT handle long rough material well; NotebookLM (Google) is excellent when the notes live in Drive and you want answers strictly from your documents. Copilot in Teams can summarise the meeting itself if your organisation has it enabled, saving the paste entirely. Check names and numbers every time; note-takers mumble and models smooth over gaps with invention.\n\n## 3. First-draft reports\n\n**Pattern A:**\n> Draft a [report type] for [audience]. Purpose: [decision it supports]. Key facts and figures: [paste or attach source material]. Structure: executive summary (max 150 words), then [sections]. Plain Australian English. Mark every figure you did not get from my material as [VERIFY].\n\n**Pattern B (from existing documents):**\n> Using only the attached documents [attach], write a draft [report type] answering: [the question]. Cite which document each major point came from. End with a list of gaps: what the question needs that these documents do not cover.\n\n*Platform notes:* Claude's Projects and ChatGPT's Projects are the right home for recurring reports because the standing material lives there. Gemini/NotebookLM shines when the sources are in Google Drive. Copilot drafts directly into Word with your templates. Pattern B's \"list the gaps\" step is the single best defence against a confident report built on thin sources, which is how the Deloitte fiasco happens at your scale.\n\n## 4. Brainstorming\n\n**Pattern A:**\n> I need ideas for [goal, e.g. getting more commercial maintenance contracts]. Constraints: [budget, region, team size, what we will not do]. Give me 15 ideas: 5 safe, 5 bold, 5 cheap-and-fast. One sentence each, then mark your top 3 with a reason.\n\n**Pattern B (the thinking partner):**\n> Let's work through [problem]. Ask me one question at a time to understand the situation before suggesting anything. After five questions, propose three approaches with the trade-offs of each.\n\n*Platform notes:* All platforms are fine here; none is magical. Pattern B turns the chatbot into an interviewer, which consistently produces better thinking than \"give me ideas,\" on every platform. Treat outputs as prompts for your own judgement, not answers; the model recombines what is common, so its \"bold\" ideas are usually medium-bold.\n\n## 5. Translating jargon into plain language\n\n**Pattern A:**\n> Rewrite this for [audience, e.g. a customer with no construction knowledge]: [paste]. Plain Australian English, short sentences, define any term you must keep. Keep every number and deadline exactly as given.\n\n**Pattern B (the reverse: explain it to me):**\n> Explain this to me like I'm smart but have never worked in [field]: [paste the contract clause / council notice / insurance letter]. What does it actually require me to do? What are the traps? Quote the exact phrases that matter.\n\n*Platform notes:* Claude tends to be strongest at careful plain-language rewriting; ChatGPT and Gemini both good; Copilot good inside your documents. Pattern B is superb on legal and insurance text, with one hard rule: it tells you what the document seems to say, a qualified person tells you what it means for you.\n\n## 6. Checking your own work\n\n**Pattern A (the cold reader):**\n> Read this as a sceptical [customer / auditor / council officer]: [paste]. List: (1) anything unclear, (2) anything that sounds like a promise we might not want to make, (3) any inconsistency in numbers or dates, (4) typos. Be blunt. Do not rewrite it yet.\n\n**Pattern B (the fact-check pass):**\n> Here is a draft: [paste]. Here are the source facts: [paste/attach]. Check every name, number, date, price and claim in the draft against the sources. Output a table: claim / supported by source? / what's wrong. Do not fix anything, just report.\n\n*Platform notes:* Pattern B is the most valuable habit in this entire sheet and works on every platform. One honest limit: the model checks your draft against your sources well, but it cannot check either against the world reliably. If a fact matters and it did not come from material you supplied, verify it yourself (or with a live-search-enabled tool, then verify anyway).\n\n---\n\n## The bottom strip\n\n- One task per chat. Fresh chat, fresh desk.\n- Paste the real material. No material, no accuracy.\n- Constraints are kindness to future-you: length, tone, format, \"if unsure, ask.\"\n- Every name, number, date and quoted rule gets checked by a human. That human is you.\n- Sensitive data: business-tier or local tools only (see Documents 3 and 5)."}, {"slug": "doc-05-data-security-basics", "sourceFile": "11-doc-05-data-security-basics.md", "title": "Document 5: Data Security Basics for AI Tools", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini and Google Workspace (Google), Copilot and Microsoft 365 (Microsoft), and local tools (Ollama, LM Studio with Llama, Mistral, Qwen or similar).\n**Written for:** someone who has never read a privacy policy. Everything here assumes zero background.\n**Last verified: 13 October 2026.** Settings menus move and policies get updated. Re-check quarterly; the review box at the end tells you how.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What happens when you type into a chatbot\n\nYou type a message. It is sent over the internet to the company's servers (usually in the United States). There, a model (a very large stack of numbers doing pattern-matching) processes your text together with everything else in the conversation, and generates a reply, which is sent back to your screen. Along the way, your text is typically: transmitted encrypted (safe from casual eavesdropping), stored in your account history (so you can see past chats), logged on the company's systems (for operations, safety review and, depending on settings and plan, possible model improvement), and potentially visible to some of the company's staff or contractors under their review policies (for example, conversations flagged by safety systems). Deleting the chat later removes it from your view and starts the company's deletion process, but it does not mean the text never existed on their systems. Treat consumer-tier chats as permanent. Treat business-tier chats as governed by contract, which is much better but still means the data left your building.\n\nWith a local tool (Ollama, LM Studio), none of the above happens: the model runs on your machine and your text goes nowhere. The trade-off is that updates, security and maintenance are your problem.\n\n## 2. \"Training on your data\", in plain words\n\nTraining means adjusting the model's numbers using text, so future versions behave differently. If a company trains on your data, your prompts and files can become part of the material that shapes the next model. What this does not mean: the model does not become a database someone can query for your client list. Regurgitation of a specific small business's documents is very unlikely. What it does mean, in practice: your business information sits in the company's systems far longer, may be read by human reviewers as part of quality and safety work, is exposed if the company is ever breached, and may subtly influence a product that your competitors also use. For most businesses the everyday risks are retention, human review and breach exposure rather than training itself, but training is the one that sets how long and how widely your data is kept, which is why the opt-outs below matter.\n\n## 3. Opt-out and defaults, platform by platform (step-by-step)\n\n**ChatGPT (OpenAI).** Consumer Free/Plus: conversations may be used to improve models unless you opt out. Steps: profile icon, Settings, Data controls, turn off \"Improve the model for everyone.\" For one-off sensitive chats use Temporary Chat (it is not used for training and disappears from history). Pro plan: not used for training. Business and Enterprise plans: business data excluded from training by default, with contractual commitments and admin controls. Retention after deletion on consumer plans is generally around 30 days and longer for data selected for training or flagged for review.\n\n**Claude (Anthropic).** Consumer Free/Pro: chats are retained for about 30 days by default. Under the consumer terms updated in August 2025, using your chats for training is an opt-in: you are asked to choose, and if you allow it, retention extends to up to five years. Steps: Settings, Privacy, and make sure the \"use my data for training\" style choice is off. If you never saw a prompt, go looking in Privacy settings anyway. Team and Enterprise plans: your content is not used to train models.\n\n**Gemini (Google).** Consumer Gemini app: your prompts are saved to your Google Account activity and may be used to improve Google products only if \"Gemini Apps Activity\" is on. Steps: gemini.google.com, Settings, Gemini Apps Activity, switch it off (this also stops the human-review pipeline for saved prompts; note Google may still keep some data for other purposes such as abuse protection). Google Workspace (paid business plans): prompts and files in Gemini features are not used to train models outside your organisation by default, and Workspace data protections apply. NotebookLM: works with sources you upload; check the same activity settings apply to your account type.\n\n**Copilot (Microsoft).** Consumer Copilot (personal Microsoft account): chats may be used to improve Microsoft's products and services unless you opt out. Steps: Microsoft account privacy dashboard (privacy.microsoft.com), manage product and service improvement settings, and in the Copilot app settings turn off conversation data sharing where offered. Work or school account (Microsoft 365 Copilot or Copilot Chat signed in with your organisational account): prompts and responses are not used to train foundation models, covered by Microsoft's commercial data protection and Data Protection Addendum. This is the single most important distinction on this page: the same-looking app behaves completely differently depending on which account you are signed in with.\n\n**Local (Ollama, LM Studio, Llama and other open models).** Nothing to opt out of; your data never leaves the machine. Your security job moves to the machine itself: keep the operating system updated, do not expose a local AI server to the internet without professional help, and remember that anyone who can use the computer can see the chats.\n\n## 4. What a Data Processing Agreement (DPA) is, and when you need one\n\nA DPA is the contract between you and a software company that says what they may do with personal information you give them: only for the service you are paying for, with defined security, with breach notification, with deletion when you leave, and with limits on their subcontractors. You need one whenever your business puts personal information (anything identifying a person: names with contact details, health information, employment records, financial details of individuals) into someone else's software. Business and enterprise tiers of all four major platforms include DPAs automatically, and that inclusion is a large part of what the higher price actually buys. Consumer tiers offer no DPA and no equivalent promise, which is why \"the free account is fine for work\" is the sentence that starts most AI data incidents.\n\n## 5. Never put these into a consumer AI tool\n\n- Passwords, API keys, login details, payment card numbers. Into any AI tool, ever, business tier included where avoidable.\n- Client lists or personal information in bulk (names plus contact details, dates of birth, tax file or Medicare numbers).\n- Client financial data: bank details, financial statements under NDA, tax records of identifiable people.\n- Health or medical information about any identifiable person.\n- Anything covered by an NDA or professional confidentiality, unless the tool's business-tier contract covers it and the NDA permits it.\n- Employee disciplinary or grievance material on consumer tools; minimised and on contracted tools only.\n- Non-public financial results, tender pricing, or pre-announcement news.\n\nIf the task genuinely requires that material, use: the business tier with the DPA, your Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace ecosystem tool with a work account, or a local model. Or redact first and un-redact in your own software afterwards.\n\n## 6. The four habits that do most of the work\n\n1. **Tier discipline.** Work happens on work accounts. Personal accounts stay personal. Check which account you are signed into before pasting, every time; the icons look alike.\n2. **The decision drill.** Before pasting: personal information? secrets? NDA? If any yes, change tool or redact.\n3. **Settings once, then quarterly.** Do the opt-outs above today, diary a quarterly re-check in the same calendar as your Document 6 pricing review.\n4. **Report same-day.** Anything sensitive that went where it should not: tell the policy owner the same day (Document 3, section 7; Document 10, scenario 1). Fast reporting turns incidents into log entries.\n\n## 7. Quarterly review box\n\n**Next review due: [date].** On review day, for each tool in use: sign in, open the privacy/data settings, confirm the training and retention toggles are still set as this document describes, check the vendor's privacy page for policy changes (each vendor publishes a \"last updated\" date), and record anything that moved in the tool register. Ten minutes per tool. This document and Document 6 are the two that go stale fastest, which is why both carry dates.\n\n## Australian context, in one paragraph\n\nIf your organisation handles personal information and is covered by the Privacy Act (most organisations over $3 million turnover, plus many smaller ones in health, personal-information trading and Commonwealth contracting), then you remain responsible for that information when it goes to an overseas AI vendor (APP 8), you must take reasonable steps to protect it (APP 11; business tiers plus a written policy are exactly such steps), an eligible data breach at your vendor can trigger your notification duties to the OAIC and affected people, and from 10 December 2026 your privacy policy must describe significant automated decision-making about people. The OAIC's generative AI guidance boils down to: minimise what you put in, secure it, be transparent, keep humans accountable. This document operationalises all four. It is practical guidance, not legal advice; for your specific situation, especially health information, talk to a privacy-savvy lawyer."}, {"slug": "doc-06-tool-comparison-matrix", "sourceFile": "12-doc-06-tool-comparison-matrix.md", "title": "Document 6: AI Tool Comparison Matrix", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**LAST VERIFIED: 13 October 2026.** Pricing and plans in this industry change every few months. This matrix is a snapshot, not scripture. Re-verify quarterly (put it in the same calendar slot as the Document 5 review). Prices below are US dollars, the global standard vendors publish; Australian-dollar prices differ (typically higher, plus GST). Always confirm on the vendor's own pricing page before budgeting.\n\n---\n\n## The matrix\n\n| | **ChatGPT (OpenAI)** | **Claude (Anthropic)** | **Gemini (Google)** | **Copilot (Microsoft)** | **Llama (Meta) local** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Free tier** | Yes, generous, rate-limited | Yes, rate-limited | Yes (consumer app, with a Google account) | Yes (consumer Copilot app/web) | The models are free; your hardware is not |\n| **Individual paid** | Go ~US$8/mo (limited markets); Plus US$20/mo (~A$31); Pro US$200/mo | Pro US$17/mo annual, US$20 monthly; Max US$100–200/mo | Google AI Pro ~US$19.99/mo; AI Ultra US$249.99/mo (heavy users only) | Copilot Pro ~US$20/mo (personal Microsoft 365 users) | $0 software; a usable 8B-class setup runs on a 16GB laptop; 12–32B models want 24–32GB memory or a 12GB+ graphics card |\n| **Business/team** | Business (formerly Team) ~US$25–30/user/mo (annual vs monthly), low seat minimum | Team ~US$25/seat/mo annual (~US$30 monthly), typically 5-seat minimum; Premium tier (~US$150) adds Claude Code allowances | Bundled into Workspace plans: Starter ~US$7, Standard ~US$14, Plus ~US$22–26/user/mo; Gemini Enterprise sold separately for larger deployments | Microsoft 365 Copilot Business ~US$21/user/mo annual commitment (Microsoft's business page also lists a US$32 \"Premium with Copilot\" tier; check which applies); Copilot Chat is included free with M365 business subscriptions (agents metered) | Free Community Licence for virtually all SMBs (licence obligations kick in only at hyperscale); Meta provides no support, you own everything |\n| **Enterprise** | Quote-only; third-party procurement reports converge around US$45–75/seat/mo | Quote-only (Team Enterprise tier ~US$20–125/seat depending on structure reported in 2026; get a quote) | Workspace Enterprise quote-only | ~US$30/user/mo annual with qualifying M365 plan | Self-hosted; your IT problem, your control |\n| **Best at** | The all-rounder: ecosystem (Projects, GPTs, memory, canvas, voice, image generation), broad task coverage, largest third-party integration world | Long documents and careful writing; Projects with persistent files; generally cautious, well-behaved output; strong coding tools (Claude Code) | If you live in Google Workspace: inside Gmail, Docs, Meet, Drive; NotebookLM for working strictly within your own documents; bundled, so no extra purchase | If you live in Microsoft 365: inside Outlook, Word, Excel, Teams with your existing permissions; work-account data protection out of the box | Total privacy: nothing leaves the building; offline use; no per-seat fees; customisable |\n| **Weak at** | Consumer tier trains on your data unless you opt out; feature sprawl can confuse; price climbs fast with add-ons | Smaller ecosystem and fewer integrations than OpenAI/Microsoft; consumer plan's training opt-in comes with 5-year retention if accepted | Consumer activity settings are a maze (see Doc 5); Workspace value depends on already paying for Workspace | Consumer Copilot and work Copilot look identical but behave differently, which trips people up; best features assume M365 subscriptions | Requires someone technical to own it; smaller models trail frontier cloud models on hard reasoning; no built-in ecosystem (your Drive/SharePoint do not appear inside it) |\n| **Trains on your data?** | Free/Plus: yes unless you opt out. Pro: no. Business/Enterprise: no (contractual) | Free/Pro: only if you opt in (default/consent prompted since Aug 2025; opt-in extends retention to 5 years). Team/Enterprise: no | Consumer: only if Gemini Apps Activity is on. Workspace business: not outside your domain | Consumer: may be used to improve services unless opted out. Work/school account: not used to train foundation models (contractual) | Nothing is transmitted anywhere; no training happens on your chats unless you do it yourself |\n| **Business DPA / admin controls** | Yes on Business/Enterprise | Yes on Team/Enterprise | Yes via Workspace terms | Yes, strongest of the group via Microsoft DPA | Not applicable (you are the processor) |\n\n## How to actually choose (the two-question method)\n\n**Question 1: What does your business already run on?** If email, files and calendar live in Microsoft 365, Copilot is the path of least resistance and best governance. If they live in Google Workspace, Gemini is the same story. If you run on neither, question 2 decides.\n\n**Question 2: What is your dominant task?** Long documents, drafting and careful rewriting point to Claude. Broad mixed use (voice, images, custom GPT-style helpers, integrations) points to ChatGPT. Hard privacy requirements or NDA-heavy work point to a local Llama-class setup or, as the pragmatic middle, business tiers of either cloud product with their contractual protections.\n\nFor a ten-person business with no strong ecosystem, the common-sense 2026 setup is: two or three seats on a business tier of one cloud product (ChatGPT Business or Claude Team, roughly US$25–30 per user per month) for day-to-day work, plus optionally one capable machine running Ollama or LM Studio with a mid-size open model for the sensitive material. Total: under A$150 per month all in, against the alternative of everyone using free personal accounts with client data, which costs nothing and risks everything.\n\n## What changes fastest (re-check these quarterly)\n\nPrices and plan names (OpenAI renamed Team to Business in 2025; expect more churn). Free-tier limits. Which models sit under each product (the engine gets swapped regularly; the car keeps its name). Data-handling defaults (Anthropic's consumer terms changed materially in August 2025; Google's activity settings have moved repeatedly). Regional availability of tiers like ChatGPT Go. The comparison of capability between cloud frontier models and the best local models (the gap narrows every year)."}, {"slug": "doc-07-meeting-email-workflow", "sourceFile": "13-doc-07-meeting-email-workflow.md", "title": "Document 7: Meeting and Email Workflow Guide", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** Microsoft 365 Copilot (Outlook, Teams, Word), Gemini in Google Workspace (Gmail, Meet, Docs) and NotebookLM, ChatGPT, Claude, and any local tool. The workflow is the same everywhere; the notes say which tool suits which step.\n**Version:** October 2026.\n\nThis is a repeatable five-step workflow: prep, capture, clean, extract, follow up. Run it the same way every time and it stops being \"using AI\" and starts being \"how we do meetings.\" Steps marked with **[HUMAN]** are where judgement must stay with a person. Those are not caveats; they are the process.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Prep (10 minutes before the meeting)\n\n**The prompt:**\n> I have a meeting at [time] with [who, their role/relationship]. Purpose: [e.g. quarterly review of the cleaning contract]. Here is the agenda: [paste]. Here are my notes from last time: [paste]. Give me: (1) a five-bullet briefing on where things stand, (2) the three questions I should make sure get answered, (3) any obvious gaps in the agenda.\n\n**Tool notes.** Microsoft 365 Copilot is strongest here if your history lives in Outlook and Teams: ask it directly (\"Prepare me for my 2pm with Bayside Dental using our past emails and the last meeting recap\") and it works across your mail and calendar with your existing permissions. Gemini does the equivalent across Gmail and Calendar in Workspace. ChatGPT or Claude work well with pasted material and are the right choice when the meeting involves sensitive commercial material and you are on their business tiers, or when you use a local tool.\n\n**[HUMAN]** You decide the three questions that actually matter. The tool's suggestions are a checklist prompt, not the strategy.\n\n## Step 2: Capture (during the meeting)\n\nOption A, automatic: Teams (with Copilot) and Google Meet (with Gemini, on eligible Workspace plans) can record and transcribe with everyone's knowledge. Turn it on only with participants' awareness; recording laws in Australia require consent of the parties, and \"the AI was listening\" is not a consent conversation to have after the fact.\n\nOption B, manual (works everywhere, including the café): keep a scratch document open and type rough notes without punctuation or mercy. Names, numbers, decisions, who-said-what matters. Do not try to write prose during a meeting; prose is Step 3's job. Voice dictation into your phone's notes app works too.\n\n**[HUMAN]** Nothing leaves the room yet. Raw notes may contain unguarded comments; that is what Step 3's edit is for.\n\n## Step 3: Clean (within a day, while memory is fresh)\n\n**The prompt:**\n> These are rough notes from [meeting, date, attendees]: [paste]. Rewrite as clean minutes: short opening line (purpose, attendees, date), then Decisions, then Discussion points (three to six bullets), then Open questions. Use only what is in the notes. Remove side comments and anything that reads as a personal remark about an attendee. Keep it under one page. Mark anything ambiguous [UNCLEAR].\n\n**Tool notes.** Claude and ChatGPT both do this well from pasted notes. Copilot in Word, or in Teams next to the transcript, keeps everything inside the Microsoft ecosystem (which matters if your business runs on it: work-account content is not used to train foundation models). Gemini in Docs, or NotebookLM with the transcript as a source, is the Google-shop equivalent. If the transcript came from Teams/Meet, Copilot/Gemini can summarise the transcript directly and you skip the paste.\n\n**[HUMAN]** Read the cleaned minutes fully before they go anywhere. Check every name, number and date. Fix the [UNCLEAR] items yourself. You are the one who was in the room; the tool was not.\n\n## Step 4: Extract actions\n\n**The prompt:**\n> From these minutes: [paste cleaned version]. Produce an action list as a table: Action / Owner / Due date / Source (which decision or discussion point it came from). If an owner or date is not stated in the minutes, write [ASSIGN] or [DATE?] instead of inventing one. Then list anything raised in discussion that has no action and no decision attached, so I can decide if it needs one.\n\n**Tool notes.** Any platform handles this; it is pure restructuring. The value is in the last sentence: the \"orphan items\" list catches the things that meetings actually lose. If actions land in your task system (Planner, Asana, Trello and friends all have AI integrations now), have the tool format the output to match how you enter tasks there.\n\n**[HUMAN]** Owners and dates get confirmed with the actual humans before the list is published. An action item assigned by a language model to someone who never agreed to it is how trust in the whole system dies in week two.\n\n## Step 5: Follow-up emails (same day or next morning)\n\n**The prompt (one per recipient, or one combined):**\n> Draft a follow-up email to [name] from [meeting]. Tone: [warm and brief / formal]. Include: thanks, the two or three decisions relevant to them, their action items with dates, and anything I owe them with my dates. Here are the minutes: [paste]. Max 120 words. Nothing that is not in the minutes.\n\n**Tool notes.** Copilot inside Outlook drafts directly in your mail with the thread and calendar context, which is the smoothest experience if you are a Microsoft shop; Gemini's \"Help me write\" in Gmail is the Google equivalent. Otherwise paste into ChatGPT or Claude and copy the result back. For the recurring follow-ups (same client, same weekly meeting), save the prompt in your platform's Project/Gem/custom instructions so Step 5 is two clicks.\n\n**[HUMAN]** Final read before send, every time, no exceptions, forever. Check the promises the email makes on your behalf: dates you can hit, prices that are current, commitments that were actually agreed. The email has your name on it, and in the Air Canada case, the organisation's name on the machine's words was enough to make the organisation liable.\n\n---\n\n## The weekly rhythm, condensed\n\n| When | What | Tool | Human check |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10 min before | Briefing and three questions | Copilot / Gemini in your ecosystem, or ChatGPT/Claude with pasted notes | You pick the real questions |\n| During | Rough capture or consented transcript | Teams/Meet transcription, or your scratch doc | Consent for recording |\n| Within a day | Clean minutes | Same ecosystem, or paste to Claude/ChatGPT | Full read; fix names, numbers, dates |\n| Same sitting | Action table plus orphan items | Any | Confirm owners and dates with people |\n| Same or next day | Follow-up emails | Outlook/Gmail in-place, or paste | Final read before send, always |\n\n## Where this workflow must not go\n\nSensitive meetings (disciplinary, M&A, health information, legally privileged): use your contracted business-tier or local tools only, or do the cleaning manually. Client-facing minutes: your edit is the deliverable, the tool's draft is scaffolding; some clients contractually restrict AI use, which your policy owner tracks (Document 3, section 4). And recording: consent first, always, in the room, out loud."}, {"slug": "doc-08-dont-panic-faq", "sourceFile": "14-doc-08-dont-panic-faq.md", "title": "Document 8: The \"Don't Panic\" FAQ", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack. For every employee, including the ones who did not ask.**\n**Version:** October 2026. Straight answers, no corporate nothing-speak.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Is AI going to take my job?\n\nParts of jobs, yes; whole jobs like yours, mostly no, and not on the timetable the headlines imply. What AI does well is produce first drafts, summaries and structured text, so the portions of your work that are routine writing and reformatting will get faster, and some of that time will be expected back by whoever signs your pay. What it cannot do is show up, take responsibility, read the room with an actual customer, notice that the numbers smell wrong, or be the person someone calls when it matters. The honest risk is not a robot replacing you; it is a competitor using these tools to serve customers faster while you do not, which is a reason to learn the tools rather than fear them. The people most exposed are those whose entire job is producing unverified text at volume, and even there the failures we have watched this decade (the Deloitte refund, Klarna rehiring humans) show quality collapsing when humans are removed too fast. Learn to use the tools well and check their work; that combination is the most durable position available.\n\n## 2. Can the AI see my screen?\n\nNot by default, no. A chatbot sees only what you send it: your typed message, files you attach, images you upload, and (in the ecosystem products) the emails, documents and calendar items its connectors are granted access to on your work account. Two genuine exceptions to know about: voice and screen-sharing features (if you explicitly start a voice session or share your screen with the tool, it processes what it receives), and the ecosystem assistants (Microsoft 365 Copilot and Gemini for Workspace can read across your work mail and files, which is their selling point, bounded by the permissions your IT admin sets). Nothing is quietly screenshotting you in the background of a normal chat session. The correct worry is not surveillance; it is what you voluntarily paste, which is what Documents 3 and 5 are about.\n\n## 3. If I put my client list in, does the AI company now have my client list?\n\nFor as long as their systems keep it, yes, in a real sense: it was transmitted to their servers, stored in your chat history and their logs, and handled under whatever their policy and your plan tier say. On a free consumer account that could mean retention for a period after you delete it, possible use in model improvement unless you opted out, and possible visibility to their reviewers under flagged circumstances. On a business tier it means retention governed by a contract, no training on your content, and deletion obligations when you ask or leave. On a local tool it means the list never went anywhere. What does not happen is the sci-fi version: your list does not become queryable by other users or get sold as a database. The practical answer is the one in Document 5: bulk personal information belongs in contracted tools or local tools, and nowhere else.\n\n## 4. Why does it sometimes make stuff up?\n\nBecause the machine is a pattern-matcher optimised to produce the most plausible continuation of your text, and plausible is not the same as true. When its training data contains lots of examples of what a good answer looks like but nothing reliable about your specific fact, it fills the gap with the shape of an answer: a confident sentence, a realistic-looking citation, a number in the right range. There is no internal alarm that distinguishes \"I know this\" from \"this is what that kind of sentence looks like,\" which is why the inventions arrive with total confidence. This is a structural feature of how these models work, not a bug that an update will fully delete, though newer products reduce it with search and grounding features. Your defence is mechanical: supply the real material in the prompt, instruct it to answer only from what you gave, and check every name, number, date and citation before the output leaves the building.\n\n## 5. Do I need to disclose that I used AI in my work?\n\nThere is no general Australian law requiring you to label ordinary business documents as AI-assisted, but there are four situations where disclosure or restraint is required or strongly advised. First, your contract: some client agreements restrict AI use, and breaching one is a commercial and legal problem (this is precisely how Deloitte ended up refunding a government report). Second, regulated advice: legal, accounting, medical and engineering output must be verified and owned by a qualified person, and presenting unverified AI text as professional advice can breach your professional obligations regardless of any AI-specific rule. Third, automated decisions about people: from 10 December 2026, privacy policies must describe significant automated decision-making, and if AI substantially shapes decisions about customers or employees, that is disclosable territory. Fourth, trust: when a client asks, answer honestly and specifically, because being caught implying human verification that never happened is a relationship-ending event, while \"we draft with AI tools and a qualified person checks everything\" is a perfectly respectable sentence. Default policy in one line: disclose on request, always, and proactively for advice-grade work.\n\n## 6. Is my employer reading my chats with the AI?\n\nOn tools the business provides and administers: assume yes, they can. Work accounts on Microsoft 365 Copilot, Google Workspace, ChatGPT Business or Claude Team give administrators the ability to manage, audit and in many configurations review usage, exactly like work email; that is part of what the business tier is. On your personal account on your personal phone, your employer generally cannot see the content, though on business-owned devices monitoring software is a separate question with a separate answer. The healthy rule is the same one that applies to work email: nothing on a work account or work device that you would not want your manager to read, and no work material on personal accounts (that is shadow AI, Document 3). If you are unsure what monitoring applies to you, ask; a legitimate employer can tell you their policy in two sentences.\n\n## 7. Is it safe to let AI write emails I send to customers?\n\nDrafting, yes; sending unchecked, no. AI is genuinely good at producing a competent first draft of a customer email faster than you can, and the quality gap between its draft and yours often favours it on routine messages. The failure modes are specific and checkable: it can invent a promise (a discount, a deadline, a warranty), get a name or number wrong, or strike a tone that reads as off for your relationship with that customer. Air Canada learned the hard way that an organisation owns what its AI tells customers, so the rule is one read-through before send, checking promises, figures and names against reality. Keep a human in the loop on anything consequential (complaints, money, cancellations, anything a lawyer might one day read aloud) and treat the tool as a fast typist, not your account manager.\n\n## 8. Will these tools keep getting smarter until they run everything?\n\nThey will keep improving; the pace and ceiling are genuinely unknown, and anyone quoting you a confident date for machine-run everything is selling something. What is observable is that progress comes in jumps on some tasks (coding, document work) and stalls on others (reliability, long-horizon judgement, the last mile of verification). Even a dramatically smarter tool stays a tool in the sense that matters to you: someone must decide the objective, supply the context, own the output and answer for the consequences, and businesses, professions and the law will keep requiring a responsible human at that point. For your working life, the sensible posture is the same one every trade took toward power tools: learn it, respect what it can do, keep your hand on the switch. The people displaced by power drills were not the ones who picked them up.\n\n## 9. What can I actually trust it to do today, without babysitting every line?\n\nLow-stakes transformation tasks where you will see the result anyway: reformatting, summarising for your own eyes, brainstorming, plain-language translation of jargon, first drafts you are going to rewrite. Everything with a number, a name, a date, a legal rule or a customer attached needs the check, and the check is usually faster than doing the task from scratch, which is why the honest productivity claim is \"faster with review\" rather than \"done for you.\" A good mental test: if this output were wrong, would anyone be harmed, embarrassed or out of pocket? If yes, it gets human eyes; if no, let it rip and enjoy the time saved. That test, applied consistently, is a complete personal AI policy.\n\n## 10. Everyone at work is using it and I'm behind. How do I catch up this week?\n\nOne hour, spread across three evenings, does it. Evening one: create an account on the tool your workplace approves (ask who that is; if nobody knows, that is your Document 3 conversation), paste in the system prompt template from Document 1 for your type of business, and run one real task through it: tomorrow's email, today's messy notes. Evening two: take the cheat sheet (Document 4) and do one task from each of the six patterns on real work material, noticing which two are worth keeping. Evening three: do the settings pass from Document 5 on your account, then set up one Project or Gem with your three most-reused reference documents in it. After that, the habit is the skill: use it once a day on something you were going to do anyway, and check the output like you would check a keen apprentice's work, because that is exactly what it is."}, {"slug": "doc-09-custom-instructions-memory", "sourceFile": "15-doc-09-custom-instructions-memory.md", "title": "Document 9: Custom Instructions and Memory: Setup Guide", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini (Google), Copilot (Microsoft), and local tools (LM Studio, Ollama).\n**Version:** October 2026. Menus move; if a step below does not match your screen, look for the nearest label in Settings containing \"personalisation\", \"memory\", \"profile\" or \"context\".\n\n---\n\n## First, what \"memory\" actually is (thirty seconds of honesty)\n\nWhen these products say the AI \"remembers you,\" here is the mechanism: the product stores ordinary text about you, either facts it extracted from your conversations (\"runs a plumbing business in Geelong; prefers short emails\") or notes you wrote yourself, and it pastes that text into the system prompt area of your future conversations. That is all. The model's numbers are not being adjusted by your chats in the moment; nothing is \"learning you\" the way a person does. Memory is a saved briefing note that gets replayed, which means you can read it, edit it and delete it like any other text, and you absolutely should, because stale notes (\"I'm preparing the March launch\") quietly distort future answers. Two separate features hide behind the word: **custom instructions** (text you write deliberately, always applied) and **memory** (text the product writes from your conversations, applied automatically). Set up the first with care; supervise the second.\n\n---\n\n## ChatGPT (OpenAI)\n\n**Custom instructions (yours, deliberate).** Web: click your profile name or icon (bottom left), Settings, Personalisation, Custom instructions. You will see two boxes. Box one, \"What would you like ChatGPT to know about you or your work?\": put identity and context (business, role, location, the systems you use). Box two, \"How would you like ChatGPT to respond?\": put your standing rules; paste the matching template from Document 1 here. Save. Mobile: menu icon, your name at top, Personalisation and Custom instructions, same two boxes.\n\n**Memory (it writes, you supervise).** Settings, Personalisation, Memory. Two mechanisms live here: \"Saved memories\" (explicit facts it decided to keep; each appears as a line of text with a bin icon beside it) and a \"Reference chat history\" style toggle that lets it draw on past conversations. To review: open Memory settings and read the saved list; you will see plain sentences, which proves the point about what memory is. To delete: bin icon per memory, or \"Clear all memories\". To stop it forming: switch the memory toggles off. To make it save something deliberately, say in chat \"Remember that our quotes are valid for 30 days\" and check it appears in the list.\n\n**Projects.** Sidebar, New project. Each project has its own instructions box and file area, and its chats can be excluded from the memory of the rest of your account depending on settings. Best practice for work: one project per recurring domain (Quotes, Marketing, Admin), instructions pasted from Document 1, standing reference files added per Document 2.\n\n**Consumer-tier warning:** if you are on Free or Plus, also check Settings, Data controls, and turn off model training. On Business/Enterprise plans, memory and training are governed by the organisation's admin settings and the contract.\n\n## Claude (Anthropic)\n\n**Profile / personal preferences (yours, deliberate).** Web or app: your initial or name (top corner), Settings, Profile. In the field asking what personal preferences Claude should consider, paste your identity lines and rules (Document 1 template). This applies across your chats.\n\n**Projects (the workhorse).** Sidebar, New project. Open the project, then its settings: a custom instructions field (paste the full Document 1 template for that context) and a Project knowledge area where uploaded files persist and are available to every chat in the project. Claude's Projects are the cleanest \"separate brain per business context\" setup of the major products: one project per client type, one per recurring task.\n\n**Memory.** Claude offers memory features on some plans (it can build summaries of past conversations and carry context forward). Look under Settings for Memory or in a chat's tools menu. The supervision rules are identical: open it, read what it stored as plain text, delete what is stale, and be aware that on consumer plans the training and retention choices from Document 5 interact with how long anything is kept (default retention about 30 days; up to five years if you opted in to training, so do not opt in at work). On Team/Enterprise plans, memory behaviour is controlled by your organisation's admin and your content is not used for training.\n\n## Gemini (Google)\n\n**Personal context (yours, deliberate).** gemini.google.com or the app: Settings (gear), look for Personal context / \"What should Gemini know about you\" (Google has also labelled this Saved info). Paste a condensed version of your template: the field favours brevity, so identity plus your five most important rules.\n\n**Gems (standing assistants).** Sidebar, Gems (or gemini.google.com/gems), New Gem. Name it (\"[Business] assistant\"), paste the full Document 1 template into its instructions. Every conversation started inside that Gem follows the template. Create one Gem per major context; this is Google's equivalent of Projects and, for business use, the better half of Gemini. Workspace users: depending on plan, Gems and the same personal context are available inside Gmail, Docs and Meet.\n\n**Memory / activity.** Gemini's ability to draw on your past chats and your Google apps is governed by Gemini Apps Activity (Settings, then the Activity page): if it is on, prompts are saved to your Google Account and can inform personalisation and, per Document 5, may feed product improvement; if it is off, chats are not saved to your account history. Review what is stored on the Activity page, delete individual items or bulk-delete there, and for work purposes the recommended setting is off unless your Workspace admin has set the business defaults, in which case Workspace protections apply and consumer-style activity concerns mostly do not.\n\n**NotebookLM (bonus, and excellent).** notebooklm.google.com: create a notebook, upload your sources (documents, PDFs, links), and every answer is grounded strictly in those sources with citations you can click. For \"answer only from my documents\" work (policies, contracts, manuals), it is the strongest consumer-accessible tool in Google's range, and for business use sits under your Workspace account.\n\n## Copilot (Microsoft)\n\n**Consumer Copilot (personal Microsoft account).** In the Copilot app or copilot.microsoft.com: Settings, Personalisation, and enter your preferences; there is also a Memory-style feature storing facts from your chats, reviewable and deletable in the same settings area, plus conversation history management under Microsoft privacy dashboard (privacy.microsoft.com). As per Document 5, check the product-improvement/data-sharing toggles here too.\n\n**Microsoft 365 Copilot (work account).** Individual personalisation is limited by design. The organisation-level equivalent of a system prompt is configured by your IT admin (organisational prompts and custom agents via the Microsoft 365 admin centre and Copilot Studio), and \"memory\" of your work context comes instead from the Microsoft Graph: Copilot can see the mail, files, chats and calendar you can see, under the permissions already in place. What an employee should do: send your Document 1 template to your admin and ask whether it can be applied organisation-wide; then in day-to-day use, rely on the fact that with a work account your prompts are not used to train foundation models. Reviewing \"what it knows\" in this ecosystem means reviewing your actual Microsoft 365 permissions with your admin, which is a good idea regardless of AI.\n\n## Local tools (Ollama, LM Studio)\n\nThere is no vendor memory because there is no vendor; persistence is whatever you set up. In LM Studio: the right-hand settings panel of the chat screen has a System prompt box; fill it with your Document 1 template, then save it as a preset so every new chat loads it. Chat history is stored locally on your machine (and you can delete it like any file). In Ollama: system prompts are set per session, via a Modelfile, or through whatever front-end app you use. \"Memory\" beyond that means your own discipline: keep your reference documents in a folder, paste or attach what you need per session, and your machine stays the only place any of it exists. That is the entire privacy appeal in three sentences.\n\n---\n\n## The maintenance routine (put it in your calendar)\n\n**Monthly, five minutes:** open the memory/review screen of each tool you use (ChatGPT: Settings, Personalisation, Memory. Claude: Settings, Memory/Profile. Gemini: Activity page. Copilot: personalisation settings) and delete anything stale or wrong. Read it as what it is: a briefing note written by a very literal intern.\n\n**Quarterly, with your Document 5 and 6 review:** re-read your custom instructions against how your business actually works now; update the templates; check the training and retention toggles have not been reset by an app update; on shared or admin-managed tools, confirm the organisation's settings still match your policy (Document 3).\n\n**One rule above all:** memory features store text about you in the vendor's systems on consumer tiers. Anything you would not want in that note (a health detail you mentioned once, a client's name, a financial worry) should not be said to a consumer tool in the first place. The delete button is a mop; the decision drill is the tap."}, {"slug": "doc-10-incident-response-card", "sourceFile": "16-doc-10-incident-response-card.md", "title": "Document 10: Incident Response Quick Card", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack. One page. Print it, pin it where the coffee machine is.**\n**Business:** [name]. **Policy owner / first call:** [name, mobile]. **Second call:** [name]. **Incident log:** [location].\n**Version:** October 2026. Calm, procedural, no drama. Same-day reporting is always the right move.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 1: An employee realises they pasted sensitive data into an AI tool\n\n*(Client details, financials, health information, passwords, anything under NDA.)*\n\n1. **Stop using that chat. Note the facts:** which tool, which account (personal or work), roughly what data, what time. Two minutes, write it down.\n2. **Tell the policy owner the same day.** There is no penalty for same-day reporting. Every hour of delay is the only thing that turns a log entry into an incident.\n3. **Delete the conversation** in the tool. On consumer tools also: check Settings for the training/opt-out toggles (Document 5) and note whether they were on. If the tool offers a data-deletion request (most vendors have one in privacy settings or support), the policy owner submits it.\n4. **If a password, key or login was pasted: change it immediately**, before anything else on this list. Assume it is compromised. Do not wait for the assessment.\n5. **Assess (policy owner, same day):** Was it personal information covered by the Privacy Act? Could it cause serious harm to anyone? Is the tool a consumer tier (worse) or contracted business tier (contained by DPA)? Most cases end here as \"logged, contained, coaching.\"\n6. **If the assessment says possible serious harm:** treat it as a potential notifiable data breach. The policy owner (with legal advice if needed) assesses against the Notifiable Data Breaches scheme, and if it is eligible, notifies the OAIC and affected individuals. Fast, factual notification is far less damaging than a late discovery.\n7. **Log it** (one line: date, tool, category of data, actions taken, outcome) and fix the pattern, not the person: usually the fix is approved tools, the decision drill, or one toolbox talk.\n\n## Scenario 2: A client asks whether AI was used in their deliverable\n\n1. **Answer honestly, specifically and without embarrassment.** Model sentence: \"We use AI tools for drafting and summarising, on contracted business platforms where our data isn't used to train their models. A qualified person on our team checked every figure and claim before this reached you. Here is our policy.\" Attach or offer Document 3.\n2. **Check the contract before answering anything beyond that.** If the engagement restricts AI use, the policy owner reviews what was done against what was promised, and we address any gap proactively. Silence and hope is not a strategy; Deloitte's refund is the case study.\n3. **Never claim checks that did not happen.** If the deliverable was not human-verified, say what was actually done. A smaller honest claim survives; a large false one ends relationships and can breach consumer law.\n4. **Log the question** (date, client, answer given). Repeated questions from clients are market research: they tell you the disclosure paragraph belongs in your standard engagement letter. Ask your lawyer to add one.\n5. **Feed it back:** if the question exposed a gap (a tool off the approved list, an unverified draft that went out), run the relevant checklist here and update the policy.\n\n## Scenario 3: A data breach involving an AI vendor is reported in the news\n\n1. **Establish the facts before forming an opinion:** which vendor, which product and tier, what data category, what period, is it confirmed or rumour. Vendor security pages and status pages first, news second.\n2. **Check whether we are affected:** does anyone here use that product? Which tier are we on (consumer or contracted business)? Did our usage in the affected window involve personal or sensitive information? The tool register (Document 6 process) makes this a ten-minute job, which is why the register exists.\n3. **If we are affected, in scope and on a business tier:** the vendor's DPA obligations kick in. The policy owner contacts the vendor's support/security channel in writing, asks the direct questions (was our data in scope, what was exposed, what remediation, will you notify us formally), and keeps the thread.\n4. **Assess our own notification duties:** under the Notifiable Data Breaches scheme, an eligible breach at our processor can still be our notification problem. If personal information we control was involved and serious harm is likely, get advice promptly and notify the OAIC and affected people. Early, plain-language notice to affected clients beats them reading about it.\n5. **Take protective actions regardless:** where feasible, rotate any credentials or keys that touched that vendor; review what we send them going forward; consider pausing sensitive use until their report lands.\n6. **If we are not affected:** log the check anyway (one line), and use it as the quarterly reminder to verify the register is current. Next incident, you will be glad it exists.\n\n## Scenario 4: AI-produced output was factually wrong and it went to a customer\n\n*(A wrong price, a fake deadline, an invented policy, a hallucinated citation in something that was sent, published or filed.)*\n\n1. **Contain:** if the wrong output is still live (website, portal, scheduled posts), pull it now. If it was sent, note exactly who received it and when.\n2. **Correct to the customer promptly and plainly.** Model: \"An error in the [quote/summary/report] we sent on [date]: [the specific error]. The correct position is [fact]. We have corrected the attached version.\" Own the error as the business; do not blame the software to the customer. Nobody has ever been reassured by \"our AI said so.\" Air Canada tried that in a tribunal and lost.\n3. **Assess the damage honestly:** did anyone act on the wrong information (pay, sign, commit, rely)? Money or commitments affected means the policy owner plus, if the amount or exposure is material, your lawyer or insurer, same week not same quarter.\n4. **Find the hole in the process, not the villain.** The standard post-mortem questions: was the material supplied to the tool (if not, it invented to fill the gap)? Did the human check step happen (Document 4, Pattern B; Document 7, step 3 and 5)? Was the output type one our policy says needs second eyes? The failure is nearly always a skipped step, and skipped steps are fixable with a checklist, not a lecture.\n5. **Fix and log:** add the specific check to the relevant workflow, run a five-minute toolbox talk if the pattern could repeat elsewhere, and log the incident (date, what went out, correction, process fix). Two or three logged incidents of the same shape tell you exactly which workflow needs redesign; that log is one of the most useful documents a small business can keep.\n\n---\n\n**The four scenarios share one rule: speed beats perfection.** Same-day telling, plain-language correcting, and written follow-up resolve almost everything at small scale. What escalates small problems is delay, hiding, and improvising answers under pressure, which is why this card exists and why the first call on it is a person, not a search engine."}], "extras": [{"slug": "facilitator-notes", "sourceFile": "17-facilitator-notes.md", "title": "Facilitator Notes", "subtitle": "", "md": "**AI Without the Sales Pitch. One page. For whoever delivers the classes.**\n\n---\n\n## Suggested pacing (per class, 90 minutes with a break, or trim to 60)\n\n- 0:00 to 0:10: Welcome, show of hands (\"who has used one of these tools this month\"), promise of the series. Never open with definitions; open with their lives.\n- 0:10 to 0:45: Outline points 1 to 6, delivered as talk plus live screen. Show, do not describe: have the tool open and do one real thing while you make the point.\n- 0:45 to 0:55: Break. Talk to people. The best material for later classes comes from break-time questions.\n- 0:55 to 1:10: Outline points 7 to 12 plus the myth to bust. Deliver the myth last among the content; it is the memory hook.\n- 1:10 to 1:25: The practical exercise. Circulate. Pairs, not solo; solo attendees disengage at keyboards.\n- 1:25 to 1:30: Debrief, one-line takeaway, homework (install the relevant document from the pack this week), next class teaser.\n\nClass-specific timing notes: Class 3's settings safari needs everyone logged in, so send a \"bring your device and your login\" reminder 48 hours ahead. Class 5's demo must be rehearsed twice and have a pre-built fallback file, because live AI builds fail on stage with cruel regularity and the failure is only funny the first time.\n\n## Common questions to expect (and the honest one-liner)\n\n- \"Which one is best?\" → \"Best at what? Your existing ecosystem decides more than any benchmark. Doc 6, two-question method.\"\n- \"Is it free though? What's the catch?\" → \"The catch is your data on consumer tiers, and that is Class 3.\"\n- \"Will it take my job?\" → Document 8, question 1, out loud, no flinching. Audiences respect a straight answer here more than anywhere else.\n- \"My nephew says the new model is conscious / AGI is next year.\" → Class 1: a stack of numbers doing pattern-matching; consciousness claims are metaphors doing unpaid work.\n- \"Can I trust it for my tax return?\" → It can explain, draft and organise; a registered agent lodges. Fluent is not verified.\n- \"What does it cost to set all this up properly?\" → Class 6 rollout: a couple of business seats, one page of policy, one week. Under A$150 a month for a ten-person business.\n- \"Someone in my office is already pasting client stuff into ChatGPT.\" → That is the amnesty conversation in Class 6; hand them Document 3 tonight, not next month.\n\n## Handling \"but I heard that...\" objections\n\nThe pattern is always the same: a headline, a podcast clip or a brother-in-law, remembered with more confidence than detail. Four moves, in order.\n\n1. **Ask for the source and date.** \"Where did you hear that, and roughly when?\" Half of objections dissolve here, because the claim predates three model generations or comes from a vendor with something to sell. This is Class 2's field test applied to the objector, gently.\n2. **Separate the capability claim from the timeline claim.** \"AI can write a whole app\" may be true at some level today, for some definition of app, with some supervision; \"AI will write all software by next year\" is a future-tense marketing sentence. Concede the first, examine the second.\n3. **Concede the kernel of truth.** Every persistent myth grew around something real. \"AI makes stuff up\" is true and is our whole checking discipline. \"AI will change work\" is true and is Class 5. Agreeing with the kernel buys you the room for the correction.\n4. **Offer the test, not the argument.** \"Bring the claim to next week and we will run it live on your own data.\" Nothing converts a sceptic like watching their own document get summarised well, or their own doomsday claim fail the pilot test. Never win an argument with a person in front of a room; win them a demonstration.\n\nSpecific recurring ones: \"But I heard ChatGPT is being sued for copyright\" (true, unresolved, and about the vendors' training data, not about whether you may use the output for your quote emails; watch this space, buy business tiers with indemnity-style terms if it matters to you). \"But I heard these tools use enormous amounts of water and power\" (true at industry scale, a legitimate reason for the hybrid approach and for not asking a datacentre to do what your laptop can). \"But my competitor says AI cut their costs 40%\" (Class 2, verbatim: whose measure, verified how; Klarna is the standing case study).\n\n## Room craft\n\n- Demo with the free tiers most attendees actually have, then mention what the paid tier changes. Never demo on Enterprise features nobody in the room will ever touch.\n- Keep a printed Doc 4 and Doc 10 on every table. People take paper.\n- When someone shares a horror story, thank them and use it. The horror stories are the curriculum; every one of them maps to a checklist in the pack.\n- Watch the clock on Class 5; the build exercise always runs long because debugging is public and debugging is slow. Pre-bake the fallback.\n- Local government attendees will ask about procurement and records obligations; point them to Class 6's vendor questions plus their own records officer. Do not freelance legal answers.\n\n## Legal line for facilitators\n\nSay this once per class: \"Everything in this course is practical guidance, not legal advice. For your specific situation, especially health information or contracts, talk to a lawyer or privacy professional.\" Then keep teaching. The disclaimer protects you; it does not excuse vagueness."}, {"slug": "research-notes-and-sources", "sourceFile": "18-research-notes-and-sources.md", "title": "Research Notes and Sources", "subtitle": "", "md": "**AI Without the Sales Pitch. What was verified, when, and where it came from.**\n**Research date: 13 October 2026.** This file underpins Documents 5 and 6 and Classes 2 and 3. Re-verify quarterly.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Pricing and plan structures (verified 13 October 2026, web research)\n\n**ChatGPT (OpenAI).** OpenAI's pricing page confirms Free plus paid plans (Go, Plus, Business, Enterprise) priced per user per month. Third-party trackers (CloudZero, Sep 2026; alloypress, Jul 2026; dancumberlandlabs, Oct 2026; coworker.ai, Jul 2026) converge on: Go ~US$8/mo (select markets), Plus US$20/mo, Pro US$100–200/mo by usage tier, Business (formerly Team) ~US$25–30/user/mo, Enterprise quote-only with procurement reports converging on US$45–75/seat/mo with seat minimums. Note: \"Team\" was renamed \"Business\"; older articles still say Team.\n\n**Claude (Anthropic).** claude.com/pricing (accessed via search): Free; Pro US$17/mo annual or US$20 monthly; Max from US$100/mo (US$200 higher tier); Team ~US$25/seat/mo annual (5-seat minimum typical) with a Premium tier (~US$150/seat) adding Claude Code allowances; Enterprise quote-only. A 2026 Anthropic solutions page references a US$20/seat annual structure with a 20-seat minimum and retired plan names, confirming churn in naming; always quote the live pricing page.\n\n**Gemini / Google Workspace.** Google's Workspace pricing and knowledge-base pages confirm Gemini features are bundled into Workspace plans (the separate \"Gemini for Workspace\" add-on era ended in 2025). Third-party (eesel.ai, Jun 2026): Business Starter ~US$7, Standard ~US$14, Plus ~US$22–26/user/mo; Enterprise quote-only. Consumer: free Gemini app; Google AI Pro ~US$19.99/mo; AI Ultra US$249.99/mo (Google's own AI plans page).\n\n**Microsoft Copilot.** microsoft.com/copilot/pricing/business (current): lists US$32/user/mo annual for a \"Premium with Copilot\" tier; third-party trackers (gosearch.ai, Sep 2026) report Copilot Free US$0, Copilot Pro ~US$20/mo, Microsoft 365 Copilot Business ~US$21/user/mo annual commitment (up to 300 users), Enterprise ~US$30/user/mo annual with qualifying M365 plan, and Copilot Chat included free with M365 business subscriptions (metered agents extra). Microsoft's support pages confirm the free consumer Copilot at copilot.cloud.microsoft and the distinction from paid Microsoft 365 Copilot. Because Microsoft's own pages show multiple figures for similarly named offers, Document 6 carries a range and a verify warning.\n\n**Meta Llama (local).** Open weights under the Meta Community Licence, free for businesses below the hyperscale usage threshold; no per-seat cost; hardware is the cost. See local AI section below.\n\n## 2. Data-handling policies (verified 13 October 2026)\n\n- **OpenAI:** enterprise-privacy page (Jan 2026) states business data is not used for training by default. Multiple privacy trackers (text.com Jun 2026; sonomos.ai May 2026; strac.io) confirm: consumer Free/Plus trained on by default unless opted out (Settings, Data controls); Pro excluded; Business/Enterprise excluded by default with DPAs.\n- **Anthropic:** \"Updates to Consumer Terms and Privacy Policy\" (anthropic.com, Aug 2025) confirms training on consumer data moved to an opt-in with a consent prompt, and opting in extends retention from ~30 days to up to 5 years. Anthropic privacy centre (2026) confirms the 30-day default and 5-year opt-in retention; Team/Enterprise excluded from training.\n- **Google:** Workspace knowledge base confirms Workspace prompt data protections (not trained on outside the domain; 18-month default retention for certain Gemini app data). Ars Technica (Apr 2026) documents the consumer settings maze: training/product improvement on consumer prompts requires Gemini Apps Activity to be on; users must find and disable it themselves.\n- **Microsoft:** learn.microsoft.com (Aug 2026) confirms Microsoft 365 Copilot prompts/responses/Graph data are not used to train foundation LLMs and are covered by the Microsoft DPA (enterprise data protection) when signed in with a work account; consumer Copilot chats may be used for product improvement unless opted out (meprism.com opt-out guide, Aug 2026; whathumansask.com, Jul 2026, summarising the consumer/work split).\n\n## 3. Marketing versus reality: the named cases used in Class 2\n\n- **Deloitte Australia / Department of Home Affairs report (October 2025):** Deloitte agreed to partially refund a ~$290,000 portion (report total reported ~$440,000) after reviewers (notably Dr Christopher Rudge) found apparent AI-generated errors including fabricated references and quotations; Deloitte admitted AI use contrary to contractual terms. Sources: Fortune (7 Oct 2025), CFO Dive (14 Oct 2025), US News, AI Incident Database (#1193).\n- **Klarna:** early-2024 claim that its AI assistant did the work of 700 customer-service agents; May 2025 public reversal by CEO Sebastian Siemiatkowski, resuming human hiring for quality. Forrester's \"AI washing\" framing (attributing financially motivated cuts to technology) is cited via reporting referenced in search results.\n- **Air Canada chatbot:** BC Civil Resolution Tribunal decision (Feb 2024) held the airline liable for its chatbot's wrong bereavement-fare information; the \"separate legal entity\" defence failed. (Pre-dates the 12-month window; used as a foundational case with its date stated.)\n- **SEC \"AI washing\" enforcement (March 2024):** Delphia fined US$225,000 and Global Predictions US$175,000 for false claims about AI use. (Foundational case, dated as such; FTC/ACCC/ASIC scrutiny of AI claims continues per klover.ai enterprise analysis, Sep 2026.)\n- **McDonald's IBM drive-through AI trial ended (June 2024)** after viral order hallucinations. (Foundational case, dated.)\n- **\"PhD-level\"/AGI-timeline claims** (OpenAI and peers, 2025–2026) used as the live future-tense example, framed as roadmap language rather than today's capability.\n\nWhere cases pre-date the \"last 12 months\" window they are explicitly dated in the class copy, because the teaching value outweighs recency and the class promises honesty about dates.\n\n## 4. Local AI state of play (2025–2026)\n\n- Hardware rules of thumb (sitepoint.com definitive guide, Feb 2026; pinggy.io, Jun 2026; iproyal.com, Aug 2026; localllm.in): ~7–9B open models usable on 16GB machines including Apple Silicon unified memory; 12–32B models want 24–32GB memory or a 12GB+ VRAM GPU; CPU-only Windows laptops run small models slowly but workably.\n- Tooling: Ollama and LM Studio remain the accessible entry points (install, select model, chat); vLLM/Jan noted for advanced users. Open-weight families cited: Meta Llama (3.1/4 generation), Qwen, Mistral, Gemma.\n- Limitations as taught: capability gap versus frontier cloud models, no native ecosystem integrations, one-person maintenance burden, sharing across an office requires server setup.\n\n## 5. Australian Privacy Act implications\n\n- **Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2024 (first tranche):** staged commencement; stronger OAIC powers, higher civil penalties, children's online privacy code process (OAIC APP Guidelines update confirmed via OAIC LinkedIn, 2025).\n- **Statutory tort for serious invasions of privacy:** in force since 10 June 2025; no turnover test.\n- **Automated decision-making transparency (APP 1):** from **10 December 2026**, privacy policies must describe, in general terms, significant automated decision-making including AI-shaped decisions (OAIC consultation on ADM guidance, May 2026; xcdit.com.au and responsibleaiaustralia.com.au compliance notes, 2026).\n- **APP 8 (cross-border disclosure)** and **APP 11 (security)** applied to chatbot use as taught in Class 3; **Notifiable Data Breaches scheme** applied to vendor breaches in Document 10.\n- **Small-business exemption:** still present; removal flagged in future reform tranches (second tranche not yet legislated as at research date). Course advice: do not build a strategy on the exemption.\n- **OAIC generative AI guidance:** minimise, secure, be transparent, keep humans accountable; cited as the plainest official statement of \"reasonable steps.\"\n- All Privacy Act content in the package is framed as practical guidance with an explicit not-legal-advice line (facilitator notes).\n\n## 6. Currency warning\n\nPricing figures, plan names (OpenAI's Team→Business rename; Anthropic's tier-name churn; Microsoft's multiple similarly priced offers) and settings paths in Documents 5, 6 and 9 will drift. Both time-sensitive documents carry \"last verified: 13 October 2026\" headers and quarterly review instructions. The conceptual content (Classes 1 to 6, Documents 1 to 4, 7, 8, 10) is deliberately built on mechanics that change slowly."}]};
